\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Option Views: G7 Words Aside, Expect More Volatility  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,139 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 05:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Russell Floyd \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Group of Seven industrial nations may have warned against "excess volatility" in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets - but don't bet on calm trading anytime soon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 For the options market, which headed into the G7 weekend with an extraordinary degree of uncertainty, long volatility trades show continued expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will be highly unstable in the near term.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 criticized excess fluctuations - interpreted by some as a call to limit the dollar's fall against the euro. But the group also reiterated a call for greater flexibility in exchange rates. In September, when the G7 first urged greater \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility, that was viewed as a reference to China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar and to some extent, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In the weeks ahead, traders will be trying to figure out how far central banks will go to damp \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility and to encourage Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 Euro-yen and cable gamma are still favored, as is holding back-end dollar-yen vega while selling small amounts of gamma in the front of the curve and running a short dollar-yen delta.  \par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, running long EUR/JPY gamma against short USD/JPY gamma was suggested. Although USD/JPY implied volatilities have become significantly more expensive (with the one-month gaining 2 percentage points in the last month), at a spread of around 7.5 vols, the actual behavior of the two pairs over the last month has been overwhelming in favor of EUR/JPY.  \par \par \uc2 One-month EUR/JPY volatility moved briefly above EUR/USD volatility last week, tradiumulative negative gamma), setting the short strike in the path of spot, say, around 104.5.  \par \par \uc2 Around 0405 GMT Monday, the dollar was at Y105.70.  \par \par \uc2 Alternatively, one could capture the behavior by selling a series of two-week straddles, resetting the strikes depending on the spot view.  \par \par \uc2 In general, front-dated USD/JPY options are priced far beyond actual spot movement, so if the USD/JPY pattern is sustained for a while longer, the cost of longer-term long-volatility trades can be reduced significantly by selling some short-dates against it.  \par \par \uc2 We would also be inclined to stick with long cable gamma. The last month has produced historical sterling-dollar volatility of around 12.15%. That's abnormally high for the pair, and can be taken advantage of until signs emerge that things are slowing down.  \par \par \uc2 GBP/USD might also be a good pair to begin to lock in volatility profits by selling strangles, or legging call or put spreads, on spot and vol moves. One-month GBP/USD volatility was 10.20% on Friday. That's quite expensive at 2.5 standard deviations above the one-year mean at 8.5%, but quite reasonable relative to the actual performance.  \par \par \uc2 At some point, volatility in this pair will come off from these levels, and profits can be locked in while leaving very low-risk directional exposure by selling some out-of-the-money strikes.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Truncated EUR/USD Distribution? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although long volatility is generally recommended, there are some interesting net-short EUR/USD vol trades out there right now.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the assumption is that the G7 means business and will smooth the ascent in the euro against the dollar, then an appropriate directional strategy might be long delta and short volatility.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. participation to stop the EUR/USD remains slim, but the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be drawn in if the euro should gain momentum and break its record high near $1.29. The euro may find the path to $1.30 relatively painless, but the $1.30-$1.35 area would be a little more sluggish and moves through $1.35 very tough indeed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Around 0405 GMT Monday, the euro was at $1.2716.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders expecting EUR/USD to climb back to test its highs but then stall in the face of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or related fears might consider owning slightly OTM EUR calls/USD puts and using the expectation of a cap around 1.35 to reduce the premium of the position by setting a reverse knock-out in the vicinity.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As an example, a one-month 1.2750 EUR call/USD put with a knock-out at 1.3500 costs 95 dollar points, compared to 144 for the vanilla (spot reference 1.2700).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of course, it's extremely frustrating to be correct on direction and timing only to see the option knock out while well in the money, and this trade works only if you have confidence that the realized distribution of EUR/USD will be truncated ahead of 1.35 for the next month. If the G7 is serious about containing volatility, then this could very well be the case.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another net-short EUR/USD vol trade that would take advantage of the recent run-up in front-month options and the extremely inverted nature of the volatility curve is to play for a flattening of the back end of the curve.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A few weeks ago, the six-month versus one-year section of the curve was inverting to a degree that has been rare historically, and from which the spread has tended to correct. We suggested traders wait a few weeks for further inversion before looking to sell the six-month and buy the one-year in a convergence trade. It looks like now is a good time to do so.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The spread was 0.3% in favor of the six-month on Friday, the most extreme inversion since November 2000, even slightly more inverted than the sharp spike in vols post-9/11. This calendar spread is now 4.25 standard deviations below the one-year mean of 0.02% in favor of one-year and the odds here dramatically favor a reversion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The last time the series dropped below the rolling one-year two-standard deviation band was when it traded to 0.25% for the six-month in July 2002. Over the subsequent three months, the six-month fell 0.65 vol relative to the one-year (taking the spread to 0.45% for the one-year).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Selling six-month at-the-money-forward straddles and buying one-year ATMF straddles in vega-neutral amounts (which isolates the relative volatility shifts and protects against parallel shifts) is attractive here.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Russell Floyd, rr.floyd@verizon.net \par  \par    (Russell Floyd is an independent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options strategist who may have positions in some of the products discussed above. Opinions expressed are those of the author, not of Dow Jones Newswires.) \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market Data provided by GFI Group, NY ( \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.gfigroup.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.gfigroup.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ) \par    Option Pricing by Fenics \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  provided by GFI Group, NY ( \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.gfigroup.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.gfigr