\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL ECONOMY-G7 statement leaves dollar to market's mercy.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerrard Raven  \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 23:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 9 (Reuters) - European finance ministers on Monday welcomed a statement from the Group of Seven warning about "excess volatility" in exchange rates, which was viewed as a compromise after their numerous complaints about the euro's export-damaging strength.   \par \par \uc2  Many investors felt the statement from the Group of Seven nations and reaction from European officials suggested there would be little coordinated action to stem the dollar's swift slide, which is widely blamed for hurting European exports and growth.   \par \par \uc2  Indeed, Belgian Finance Minister Didier Reynders told Reuters there had been no talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at the euro zone finance minister gathering in Brussels Monday.   \par \par \uc2  For his part, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow repeated the longstanding U.S. position that exchange rates should be left to competitive markets, though \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should never be ruled out.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar showed little reaction to all the talk. Against the euro,\par the dollar rose slightly to $1.2683  in late New York trading. Against\par the yen, the greenback climbed 0.2 percent to 105.62 yen       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Asian countries at which the statement was primarily aimed,\par meanwhile, showed reluctance to bow to the Group of Seven's demand that\par they help to smooth out current account imbalances threatening the\par global economy.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A weekend meeting of G7 finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors in\par Boca Raton, Florida concluded with a statement saying "excess\par volatility" and "disorderly movements" in exchange rates were\par undesirable.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In an attempt to pressure countries such as China to end their\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' link to the U.S. dollar, it said that where market forces\par do not influence \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  values, it was desirable to introduce such\par flexibility.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The outcome appeared a compromise between euro zone countries, which\par had gone into the meeting saying a sharp rise of the euro was hitting\par exporters and jeopardising their economic upturn, and the United States\par which insisted economic growth should be the chief agenda item.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Until the G7 actually starts making noises about absolute levels of\par euro/dollar or dollar weakness being an issue, the market will continue\par to see a green light to sell dollars," said Shahab Jalinoos, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategist at ABN AMRO.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIA UNINTIMIDATED\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The G7 call for market forces to determine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  values was seen as\par a thinly veiled reference to China, which has kept the yuan virtually\par fixed against the dollar for almost a decade, and to Asian countries\par like Japan that have bought huge volumes of dollars to hold their\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Because of Asia's rigidity, selling pressure on the dollar has\par translated principally into a sharp rise in the euro.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Speculation has been swirling that China would soon let the yuan  trade\par in a wider range. Most economists expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would rise if it\par were cut free, dampening inflationary pressures building in the Chinese\par economy.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A spokesman for the People's Bank of China (PBOC), which earlier had\par dismissed as groundless a local newspaper report forecasting a five\par percent revaluation next month, said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was indeed\par studying plans for exchange rate reform.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But he could give no timetable and said it had nothing to do with the\par stance taken by the G7. "We have been always studying plans to reform\par the exchange rate system and that's not because of the G7 meeting," he\par said.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The G7's statement showed no difference from Japan's policy,\par Vice-Finance Minister Zembei Mizoguchi told reporters in Tokyo on his\par return from Florida. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets on a\par massive scale in January, boosting its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves by\par $67.7 billion to slow the yen's ascent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The description of lacking flexibility does not fit Japan," Finance\par Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said in Boca Raton. He noted that the yen\par had swung about as much as the euro since the prior G7 meeting in Dubai\par in September.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040209e029001uj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
