\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-G7 flexibility call draws cool response in Asia.   \b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 10:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  BEIJING, Feb 9 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven's quest to unshackle Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ran into trouble on Monday when China said it was studying reforms, but not because of the G7, and Japan and South Korea said they saw no need to change their ways.    \par \par \uc2  The responses of Northeast Asia's big-three economies underlined their reluctance to relegate their national interest to the G7's demand that they do their bit to smooth out current-account imbalances that are threatening the global economy.    \par \par \uc2  In a statement issued after two days of talks in Boca Raton, Florida, G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors emphasised that more flexibility in exchange rates was desirable for major countries or economic areas that lacked such flexibility.    \par \par \uc2  The statement was a thinly veiled reference to China, which has kept the yuan virtually fixed against the dollar for almost a decade, and to countries such as South Korea and Taiwan that have bought huge volumes of dollars to hold their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down.    \par \par \uc2  Because of Asia's rigidity, selling pressure on the dollar brought about by a U.S. current account deficit exceeding five percent of gross domestic product has translated principally into a sharp rise in the euro. Europe, worried for its exporters, wants Asia to shoulder more of the adjustment burden.    \par \par \uc2  Speculation has been swirling that China would soon make good on its long-standing plan to let the yuan, or renminbi , trade in a wider range. Most economists expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would rise if it were cut free, thus dampening inflationary pressures building in the Chinese economy as a result of hot money inflows.    \par \par \uc2  A spokesman for the People's Bank of China (PBOC), which earlier had dismissed as groundless a local newspaper report forecasting a five percent revaluation next month, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was indeed studying plans for exchange rate reform.    \par \par \uc2  But he could give no timetable and said it had nothing to do with the stance taken by the G7 - the United States, Japan, Germany, Britain, France, Italy and Canada.    \par \par \uc2  "We have been always studying plans to reform the exchange rate system and that's not because of the G7 meeting," he said.    \par \par \uc2  SHORT SHRIFT    \par \par \uc2  South Korea also gave the G7 short shrift, saying it did not regard itself as one of those countries that the G7 had suggested were keeping their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on too tight a leash.    \par \par \uc2  "We judged the won \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved with considerable flexibility last year," Choi Joong-kyung, chief of the finance ministry's international finance bureau, told Reuters.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Choi chose to emphasise a warning in the G7 communique against "excess\par volatility" - a reason the Seoul authorities frequently give to explain\par their periodic forays into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to slow the rise\par of the won        .\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The communique put an emphasis both on warnings over volatility and\par flexibility. But I believe that G7 put more weight on the volatility,"\par he said.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Seoul's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves rose $2.1 billion in January to a\par record $157.5 billion, after jumping 28 percent in 2003, due largely to\par official dollar buying aimed at keeping the won competitive against\par South Korea's major competitors.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan also said it was not in the G7's crosshairs.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The G7's statement that excess volatility and disorderly movements were\par undesirable for economic growth showed no difference from Japan's\par policy, Vice-Finance Minister Zembei Mizoguchi told reporters in Tokyo\par on his return from Florida.\par    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets on an unprecedented scale in\par January, boosting its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves by $67.7 billion to\par slow the yen's ascent. Its stockpile of reserves, which grew 43.4\par percent in 2003, is now a record $741.2 billion.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The G7, however, has largely turned a blind eye to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's massive\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , reasoning that a sharp rise in the yen would undermine\par Japan's export-led recovery and deepen the deflation that is making it\par hard for firms to pay their debts.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The description of lacking flexibility does not fit Japan," Finance\par Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said in Boca Raton. He noted that the yen\par had swung about as much as the euro since the prior G7 meeting in Dubai\par in September.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040209e029001at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
