\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar at 2-wk low vs euro, G7 warning lacks bite.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlotte Cooper  \par \uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 10:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 9 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a two-week low on the euro and an 11-year trough against sterling on Monday as the market decided a Group of Seven warning against "excess volatility" in exchange rates did not herald action to support the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 In a see-saw reaction to a weekend statement from G7 finance ministers, the dollar jumped nearly one percent against the euro at the start of Asian trade but quickly reversed its gains as the market grew sceptical the G7 stance signalled intention to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has come under broad-based pressure, particularly against the euro, on the view that the U.S. current account deficit is unsustainable and the U.S. administration is happy to see it fall to correct that imbalance and boost economic growth ahead of November's presidential elections.  \par \par \uc2 "Until the G7 actually starts making noises about absolute levels of euro/dollar or dollar weakness being an issue the market will continue to see a green light to sell dollars," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London.  \par \par \uc2 By 0845 GMT the euro was at $1.2740, a quarter of a percent up from pre-G7 levels on Friday. It hit a two-week peak around $1.2760, some 1-1/2 cents below last month's record high, extending a 1.3 percent gain on Friday after disappointing U.S. jobs data reinforced expectations for continued low U.S. interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was holding at 105.60 yen, just above a three-year low set last week, and was near its 11-year low of $1.8593 per British pound set as European business opened.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the euro gained more than half a percent on the yen to trade at 134.65.  \par \par \uc2 ANALYSING THE STATEMENT  \par \par \uc2 The G7, on top of its standard communique phrases about monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets closely, said: "Excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth."  \par \par \uc2 The reference to volatility was seen as a change of emphasis to counter the impact of the G7's call in Dubai last September for "more flexibility" in exchange rates, which sparked a 10 percent drop in the dollar's value against the euro and rang alarm bells among euro zone officials.  \par \par \uc2 "Whatever monetary authorities say will only have a temporary effect since the core reason behind the dollar's weakness remains unchanged," said Katsunori Kitakura, treasury department manager at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "Until the ECB (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  or there is great improvement in U.S. deficits, the weak dollar trend will continue."  \par \par \uc2 The G7, in its statement, this time narrowed its call for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to countries "that lack such flexibility".  \par \par \uc2 Traders assumed the shift was aimed at China and other Asian countries that peg their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar, although speculation simmered over whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has engaged in massive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past year, was also under fire.  \par \par \uc2 "Some people are saying Japan was the biggest winner from this statement," Jalinoos said. "Japan seems to have got implicit ok from the other G7 players to carry on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Saturday that the statement urging more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility was not aimed at Japan.  \par \par \uc2 Zembei Mizoguchi, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, said on Monday the group's statement showed no discrepancy with Japan's policies and vice finance minister Masakazu Hayashi said the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance was unchanged and exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen ($188.8 billion) last year and another seven trillion yen last month to curb the yen's export-hurting rise. Traders expect it to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the dollar threaten to fall below 105 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040209e029001ar\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
