\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-No change in stance of acting in  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  mkt-Japan MOF.   \b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 09:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 9 (Reuters) - Japanese Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi said on Monday there was no change to Japan's stance of acting to stem excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements after a Group of Seven statement over the weekend warned against "excess volatility" in exchange rates.   \par \par \uc2 Asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the G7 meeting, Hayashi told a news conference: "It is desirable for exchange rates to reflect economic fundamentals and move stably. Our stance of taking appropriate action against excessive movements is unchanged."   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo sold a record 20 trillion yen ($188.8 billion) last year to curb the yen's rise, as well as more than seven trillion yen last month, also a record for a single month.   \par \par \uc2 Hayashi added that Japan's basic stance of seeking stable exchange rates was included in the G7 statement and that the G7 had reaffirmed and shared such views.   \par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors meeting in Florida over the weekend issued a communique saying, "Excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth."   \par \par \uc2 But the dollar hit a two-week low against the euro in Asia trade on Monday, losing early gains as the market decided the G7 statement did not herald action to support the greenback.   \par \par \uc2 The G7 also narrowed its call for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to countries "that lack such flexibility".   \par \par \uc2 Traders assumed the modification hinted that the G7 nations had China and other Asian countries that peg their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar in mind, although speculation simmered on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also under fire.   \par \par \uc2 Hayashi reiterated Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki's remarks after the G7 meeting that Japan was not targeted as a country lacking \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.   \par \par \uc2 "The yen has in fact moved broadly, it does not lack flexibility," Hayashi said. "That was confirmed at the G7 meeting." ($1=105.94 yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040209e0290019s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
