\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar at 2-week low vs euro, erases post-G7 gains.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada  \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 07:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 9 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a two-week low against the euro on Monday, wiping out early gains as the market decided the Group of Seven's warning against "excess volatility" in exchange rates did not herald action to support the greenback.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped nearly one percent against the euro at the start of trade as dealers initially took the G7 statement as a sign that Washington had paid heed to European and Japanese concerns about the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp fall.   \par \par \uc2  But it quickly reversed course and fell to a low of $1.2760 per euro on the growing perception that the G7 warning was little more than lip service and that any coordinated action to stop the dollar's two-year-long decline was unlikely.   \par \par \uc2  "Whatever monetary authorities say will only have a temporary effect since the core reason behind the dollar's weakness remains unchanged," said Katsunori Kitakura, treasury department manager at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking.   \par \par \uc2  "Until the ECB (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  or there is great improvement in U.S. deficits, the weak-dollar trend will continue."   \par \par \uc2  As of 0610 GMT, the euro was $1.2732/35, compared with around $1.27 in late New York trade on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  It rose more than one yen to a two-week high of 134.50/59 yen compared with 133.36/46.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar was flat against the yen at 105.63/68 yen, after briefly rising to a high of 105.97 yen in a knee-jerk reaction to the G7 communique. Many traders expect Japanese authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the yen rise further.   \par \par \uc2  Sterling also reversed course and pushed up to a four-week high of $1.8549. It was at 1.8521/26, versus $1.8465 in late U.S. trade on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  The G7, on top of its standard communique phrases about monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets closely, said, "Excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth."   \par \par \uc2  The reference to volatility was seen as a change of emphasis to counter the impact of the G7's call in Dubai last September for "more flexibility" in exchange rates, which had sparked a 10 percent drop in the dollar's value against the euro.   \par \par \uc2  A weak dollar makes European and Japanese exports less competitive.   \par \par \uc2  BUSINESS AS USUAL   \par \par \uc2  The G7, in its statement, also narrowed its call for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to countries "that lack such flexibility".   \par \par \uc2  Traders assumed the change hinted that the G7 nations had China and other Asian countries that peg their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar in mind, although speculation simmered on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also under fire.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Saturday that the statement urging more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility was not aimed at Japan.   \par \par \uc2  Zembei Mizoguchi, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, said on Monday the group's statement showed no discrepancy with Japan's policies.   \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should reflect economic fundamentals and move in a stable manner, in the medium term," he told reporters after returning from the G7 meeting.   \par \par \uc2  Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen ($188.8 billion) last year and another seven trillion yen last month to curb the yen's export-hurting rise. Traders expect it to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the dollar threaten to fall below 105 yen.   \par \par \uc2  "I think Japanese authorities have gotten more confident about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  since they didn't come under criticism at G7," said Hideo Kumano, senior economist at Dai-ichi Life Research Institute.   \par \par \uc2  "The focus now will be on if they will shift their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance to a more aggressive one and push down the yen." ($1=105.94 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040209e029000zu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
