\par\par \b \uc2 MOF Watanabe:G7 "Excess Volatility" Points To Euro, Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2004\par 23:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A phrase warning against "excess volatility" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, included in a Group of Seven financial officials' meeting's communique, applies to both the euro/dollar and dollar/yen, a top Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "The phrase means if there is excess volatility or inappropriate moves in the market, we will take necessary action," Hiroshi Watanabe, the head of the MOF's international bureau said.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said Japan's view was reflected in the G7 statement. "Finance Minister (Sadakazu) Tanigaki seemed satisfied with the outcome" of the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers, meeting in Boca Raton, Florida last Friday and Saturday, clarified their position on exchange rates, issuing a communique which included a reference to avoiding "excess volatility" that markets may interpret as a desire to limit further dollar declines against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "Excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth," the G7 said. "We continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate. In this context, we emphasize that more flexibility in exchange rates is desirable for major countries or economic areas that lack such flexibility to promote smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system, based on market mechanisms."  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said there was hardly any discussion at the G7 meeting on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which reached a hefty Y20 trillion in 2003 and Y7 trillion in January.  \par \par \uc2 "There wasn't any criticism (from other G7 members) of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Watanabe said, shrugging off views that the communique's call for "flexibility" in exchange rates was targeted at Japan, among other Asian nations that frequently step into the market to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said the market's "misinterpretation" of the previous G7 statement following the meeting in Dubai was corrected at the Boca Raton meeting.  \par \par \uc2 "We sent a more accurate message to the market" on the G7 nations' view on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, Watanabe said.  \par \par \uc2 At the last meeting of G7 financial officials in Dubai in September 2003, the statement's call for "flexibility" in exchange rates was widely interpreted as tacit approval for a weaker dollar, and precipitated a sharp decline in the greenback's value against the yen and the euro.  \par \par \uc2 While the Boca Raton communique's call for flexibility was aimed at "major countries or economic areas that lack such flexibility," the G7 officials didn't specify which countries they were talking about.  \par \par \uc2 -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Gibson and Bennett Richardson  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040208e0280007v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
