\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Watch: Tokyo's Victory On  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Only Partial At G7  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,122 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2004\par 11:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takeshi Takeuchi \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOCA RATON, Florida (Dow Jones)--Japan won some concessions on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy at a Group of Seven finance authorities' meeting here this weekend, but not enough to do much to halt the yen's rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The delegates from Tokyo can pat themselves on the back for at least one thing: they got other finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the G7 industrialized countries to agree to warn in their closing statement against "excess volatility and disorderly movements" in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that "are undesirable for economic growth."  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, emerging from the meeting with a bright smile befitting the Sunshine State that hosted the event, said that new sentence endorses Tokyo's long-standing policy to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the yen's moves are out of whack with economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "The new statement was intended to correct market misunderstanding" of the statement from the last G7 finance authorities' meeting in Dubai, Tanigaki told reporters after the G7 conference. "I hope \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers will read the new statement time and time again, not just once or twice."  \par \par \uc2 There's little doubt many traders will scrutinize the G7's declaration on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But Tanigaki might not like the way the market interprets the group's words.  \par \par \uc2 For weeks, financial market traders had been waiting for what the G7 policy-makers would say about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when the dollar is sliding nonstop. In Japan's case, the meeting was also a chance for the G7 to make more clear what it collectively thought of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign to keep the yen from rising.  \par \par \uc2 In a year's time, the dollar has fallen 11% against the yen, and is near three-and-a-half-year lows. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen even more sharply against the euro during that time, having lost 20%.  \par \par \uc2 Many traders are likely, though, to conclude from reading the G7 statement that North American and European counterparts still have little sympathy for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, and certainly won't do anything themselves to curb the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    A-plus In Negotiating \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It was, to be sure, a small victory for Japan to get the G7 as a whole to warn against too sharp and disorderly moves in exchange rates. Tokyo policy-makers often cite such rough moves as a reason for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Our minister gave us an 'A-plus' grade for our G7 preparations and negotiations," said a senior Japanese official.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, another addition to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  statement might prompt traders to question whether G7 partners even approve at all of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In September, the dollar began to tumble after G7 finance authorities called at their Dubai meeting for "more flexibility" in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That was interpreted by markets as a sign the U.S. wanted a stop to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that supports the dollar, and the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's absence from the market for several sessions after the Dubai meeting only added to speculation the Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy had changed as a result of the meeting. Since then, Tokyo officials have often said this reading of the statement is wrong, and indeed they proceeded to spend record amounts in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G7 repeated the "more flexibility" part in its latest statement, but this time added specifically that it wants that from "major countries and economic areas that luck such flexibility."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Could the G7's North American and European members have been thinking about Japan as one of those areas with an inflexible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, considering the huge sums Tokyo has been spending to keep the yen from rising?  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki insists not: "Other G7 countries agree that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime is flexible," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But some in the market aren't so sure - Japan, after all is the only member of the G7 - which also includes the U.S., Canada, U.K., Germany, France and Italy - that actively conducts \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with policy-members of other G7 countries saying it's better for the market to decide rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Some of us may well suspect that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with a record of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  totaling Y27 trillion for the past 13 months, is also included in such areas" that don't have flexible exchange rates, said Satoshi Tate, vice president of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at UFJ Bank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      Tug-of-war At Y105 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, it appears that heavy lobbying by European G7 delegates was a bigger factor than Tokyo's pleas in getting the U.S. to agree to changes in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  statement, said a senior official involved in the G7 negotiations.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With U.S.-Europe relations still fractured in the aftermath of their quarrel over Iraq last year, Washington didn't want to "add insult to a barely-healed injury" by flatly turning down European proposals on exchange rate policy, the source said. "The U.S. has increasingly become hesitant to push the Europeans into a corner."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe's economic growth has also been more sluggish than in the U.S. and Japan while its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has strengthened against both the dollar and the yen over the past year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the changes to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  statement are aimed mainly at Europe, the dollar could face more downward pressure against the yen rather than euro, UFJ's Tate said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For the last year, it has been the euro that has borne the biggest brunt of dollar sales against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Now, it will be the yen's turn."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In any case, yen bullishness is unlikely to fade, particularly because the G7 didn't drop the "more flexibility" phrase, as some thought it might do.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market players now feel that that expression was aimed mainly at China and some Asian emerging countries that keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates pegged to the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But many traders also feel that the expression is meant to justify a gradual weakening in the dollar to help reduce the U.S.'s current account and budget deficits, said Yasunari Ueno, chief economist at Mizuho Securities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's "authorities will surely counter such (dollar) selling with heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent the yen from rising. But for the coming weeks, it will be a tug of war between the MOF and the market at around Y105 (to the dollar)," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takeshi Takeuchi, Dow Jones Newswires; 1-202-862-9185; Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Takeshi Takeuchi is Dow Jones Newswires special writer, specializing in U.S.-Asia relations. Previously, he covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and Japanese economic and trade policy and politics for two decades in Tokyo.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1187 4256  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040208e0280003v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
