\par\par \b \uc2 G7 Clarifies  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Policy; Says Volatility Unwelcome  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever and Elizabeth Price  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 940 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2004\par 05:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BOCA RATON, Fla. (Dow Jones)--Group of Seven finance ministers clarified their position on exchange rates Saturday with a reference to avoiding "excess volatility" that markets may interpret as a desire to limit further dollar declines against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The language on exchange rates differed only a little, but crucially, from that which followed the group's last meeting in Dubai in September. That statement, which introduced a call for "more flexibility in exchange rates" triggered an acceleration in the dollar's selloff against the yen and euro. European and Japanese officials, worried the stronger euro and yen would hurt their economic recoveries, this time overrode U.S. objections and added a statement of concern about volatility for the first time in several years.  \par \par \uc2 "Excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth," the G7 said. "We continue to monitor exchange markets closely and cooperate as appropriate. In this context, we emphasize that more flexibility in exchange rates is desirable for major countries or economic areas that lack such flexibility to promote smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system, based on market mechanisms."  \par \par \uc2 Although initially preferring to leave the Dubai statement on flexibility unchanged, the U.S. eventually agreed because they too recognized that markets had misinterpreted the Dubai language - aimed at China and other fixed Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has appreciated around 14% against the dollar since the Dubai meeting. The dollar has lost less of its value against the yen, largely because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the market selling yen.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said the statement should pave the way for smoother moves in the market.  \par \par \uc2 "Everyone's happy," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York, particularly the euro-zone officials. "The Europeans will be thrilled with the language."  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Jean-Claude Trichet said, `It's a very good communique. That consensus is very clear. It clarifies the situation ... and I would say it speaks for itself."  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials were equally pleased because they often justify heavy-handed market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the need to smooth abrupt market moves.  \par \par \uc2 "The newly inserted words in the statement, such as 'excessive volatility' and 'disorderly moves,' are what I myself and Europe have been suggesting be included," said Sadakuza Tanigaki, Japan's finance minister attending his first G7 meeting in his new job.  \par \par \uc2 Only the U.S. was eager to downplay the changes, with U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow saying the new policy is consistent with the long-standing U.S. strong dollar policy.  \par \par \uc2 "This statement is entirely consistent with the strong dollar policy, which you've heard me articulate many times," Snow told reporters in a press conference after the G7 meeting. "A strong dollar is in the national interest. The relative values of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are best set in open, competitive markets."  \par \par \uc2 Snow has long been a source of pressure on the Chinese to move to a more flexible exchange-rate system.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, trades in a narrow, tightly controlled band against the dollar. The U.S. wants China to begin dismantling the peg, in part to appease U.S. exporters who complain the yuan peg makes its competitor's products artificially cheap and in part to make China more resilient to economic shocks.  \par \par \uc2 "The reference to `more flexibility in exchange rates is desirable for major countries or economic areas that lack such flexibility' is a clearly call on China and other Asian countries to allow more exchange rate strength," wrote UBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts in a research note Saturday. "But we expect the Japanese will argue that the statement does not refer directly to them - given the yen has appreciated by around 10% against the dollar since the last G7 meeting."  \par \par \uc2 By focusing the spotlight on excessive volatility and turning attention more toward China, the G7 statement might not trigger the knee-jerk reaction in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Monday, as many analysts had originally expected.  \par \par \uc2 "The G7 effectively dodged the bullet,' said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York in New York. "I think the dollar will rebound modestly next week and avoid the big selloff."  \par \par \uc2 Woolfolk reckons the euro will head back down below $1.25 from around $1.27 currently.  \par \par \uc2 In the longer-term, however, the statement does little to suggest that the dollar will reverse its weakening trend.  \par \par \uc2 "The decision to keep the language on exchange rate flexibility adopted at the September G7 meeting in Dubai, and the absence of new language suggesting exchange rate moves are already inconsistent with fundamentals, implies the United States is happy to see the dollar continue weakening - despite Treasury Secretary Snow afterwards reaffirming his support for the strong dollar policy," the UBS analysts wrote.  \par \par \uc2 The din of commentary about exchange rates almost overshadowed other topics. Once again, ministers pledged a series of individual reforms to make their economies more efficient, including promises to cut budget deficits. They renewed a vow to cooperate in shutting off terror finance, and promised to cut the costs of worker remittances to poor countries and other reforms to make the international financial system work better.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 includes the U.S., Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, and the U.K. The ministers' next meeting is scheduled in April in Washington to coincide with the International Monetary Fund and World Bank spring meetings there.  \par \par \uc2 -By Elizabeth Price and Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; elizabeth.price@dowjones.com; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040208e0280002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
