\par\par \b \uc2 ODJ Tanigaki:Japan  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View Was Reflected In G7 Statement  \b0\par\par\uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2004\par 02:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOCA RATON, Fla. (Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki \par expressed satisfaction with the Group of Seven finance ministers' communique \par Saturday, saying "Japan's view on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was reflected in the \par statement." \par  \par "The newly inserted words in the statement, such as 'excessive volatility' \par and 'disorderly moves,' are what I myself and Europe have been suggesting be \par included," Tanigaki told a post-G7 press conference. \par  \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  portion of the new G7 statement added a sentence \par that "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are \par undesirable for economic growth." Other mentions of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were \par identical with wording from the communique of the last G7 meeting in Dubai. \par  \par "In this sense, not only Japan, but also all other major areas (of the G7) \par are happy," Tanigaki added. \par  \par The Japanese finance minister stressed that the G7 delegates, in drafting the \par new statement, took extra care that it not be misinterpreted by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign- \par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, like the last statement issued after the Dubai G7 meeting. \par  \par "We share the notion that the last statement was misinterpreted by the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. We took extra care in the wording to avoid similar \par misunderstanding this time," he said. \par  \par Tanigaki was referring to the phrase that the G7 favors "more flexibility in \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates" that promotes "smooth and widespread adjustments in \par the international financial system." Market players interpreted the Dubai \par statement to mean the G7 was giving the green light to push the dollar lower. \par And \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily by buying dollars to prevent the yen's rise. \par  \par "The last statement was not aimed at Japan. In today's meeting too, every G7 \par country agreed that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime is flexible," Tanigaki said. \par  \par The euro also was subjected to similar market misunderstanding, having risen \par about 12% against the dollar since the Dubai meeting. \par  \par "So, I hope the new statement will correct the market misunderstandings of \par the last statement," the Japanese finance minister said. \par  \par Tanigaki, however, declined comment when asked about what countries the G7 \par leaders urged to adopt a more flexible exchange regime. \par  \par "It's not appropriate for me to comment," he said. \par  \par Many G7 officials, however, admit that the phrase is targeted at China and \par some Asian emerging-market countries. \par  \par Asked about whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was discussed during the G7 \par meeting, Tanikaki avoided answering directly. Instead, the Japanese finance \par minister said he explained Japan's basic stance on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy \par during the meeting. \par  \par Japan spent a record Y20 trillion in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market last year to \par prevent the yen's rise against the dollar. The Finance Ministry continued to \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily in January, buying dollars worth Y7 trillion during the \par period. \par  \par Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui, who also represented Japan at the \par meeting, said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary easing action two weeks ahead of \par the meeting was not taken in order to gain support for Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par policy from other G7 members. \par  \par "The easing step was aimed at ending Japan's deflation and helping put the \par economy back on a sustained recovery path," Fukui said. \par  \par Some G7 countries, particularly the U.S., deem \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yen-selling \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation,"  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCS000020040208e02800004\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
