\par\par \b \uc2 Tanigaki:Japan  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View Was Reflected In G7 Statement  \b0\par\par\uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2004\par 00:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BOCA RATON, Fla. (Dow Jones)--Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki expressed satisfaction with the Group of Seven finance ministers' communique Saturday, saying "Japan's view on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  was reflected in the statement."  \par \par \uc2 "The newly inserted words in the statement, such as 'excessive volatility' and 'disorderly moves,' are what I myself and Europe have been suggesting be included," Tanigaki told a post-G7 press conference.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  portion of the new G7 statement added a sentence that "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth." Other mentions of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  were identical with wording from the communique of the last G7 meeting in Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 "In this sense, not only Japan, but also all other major areas (of the G7) are happy," Tanigaki added.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese finance minister stressed that the G7 delegates, in drafting the new statement, took extra care that it not be misinterpreted by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, like the last statement issued after the Dubai G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 "We share the notion that the last statement was misinterpreted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. We took extra care in the wording to avoid similar misunderstanding this time," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki was referring to the phrase that the G7 favors "more flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates" that promotes "smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system." Market players interpreted the Dubai statement to mean the G7 was giving the green light to push the dollar lower. And \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily by buying dollars to prevent the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 "The last statement was not aimed at Japan. In today's meeting too, every G7 country agreed that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime is flexible," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 The euro also was subjected to similar market misunderstanding, having risen about 12% against the dollar since the Dubai meeting.  \par \par \uc2 "So, I hope the new statement will correct the market misunderstandings of the last statement," the Japanese finance minister said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki, however, declined comment when asked about what countries the G7 leaders urged to adopt a more flexible exchange regime.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not appropriate for me to comment," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Many G7 officials, however, admit that the phrase is targeted at China and some Asian emerging-market countries.  \par \par \uc2 Asked about whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was discussed during the G7 meeting, Tanikaki avoided answering directly. Instead, the Japanese finance minister said he explained Japan's basic stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy during the meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Japan spent a record Y20 trillion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last year to prevent the yen's rise against the dollar. The Finance Ministry continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in January, buying dollars worth Y7 trillion during the period.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui, who also represented Japan at the meeting, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary easing action two weeks ahead of the meeting was not taken in order to gain support for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from other G7 members.  \par \par \uc2 "The easing step was aimed at ending Japan's deflation and helping put the economy back on a sustained recovery path," Fukui said.  \par \par \uc2 Some G7 countries, particularly the U.S., deem \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation," of which they disapprove, unless it is accompanied by monetary expansion.  \par \par \uc2 -By Takeshi Takeuchi, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9285; Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040207e02700072\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
