\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan throws weight behind Mer's call on G7.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2004\par 03:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOCA RATON, Fla., Feb 6 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki threw his weight behind his French counterpart on Friday, saying the Group of Seven should look at the need for both stability and flexibility in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Seizing on remarks by French Finance Minister Francis Mer urging the economic powers to clarify their September statement calling for more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility - words that accelerated the dollar's fall - Tanigaki told reporters ahead of a G7 meeting here that stability was as important as flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has made no secret of its worries that a higher yen could derail its export-reliant economic recovery, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market over the past year. But its market forays have made exchange rate talks a thorny issue, and Mer's comments appeared to embolden Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "There is probably a lot of debate going on over them. But we will have to discuss both (flexibility and stability)," Tanigaki said in response to questions about Mer's comments.  \par \par \uc2 "It is desirable for exchange rates to reflect economic fundamentals and to trade in a stable manner. We will take appropriate steps if they deviate from fundamentals," he added as he headed for a dinner with finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs from the world's leading industrial countries.  \par \par \uc2 Financial markets are focused on whether the wording in a communique to be issued after Saturday's meeting will differ significantly from the prior one in September.  \par \par \uc2 Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  totaling a record 20 trillion yen last year and a further seven trillion yen in January alone, the dollar fell to its lowest level against the yen in nearly 3-1/2 years this week.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also plumbed record lows against the euro last month, prompting European officials to publicly fret about the negative impact on the area's exports.  \par \par \uc2 LONG-TERM SOLUTION  \par \par \uc2 Many in the market believe that a minor change in the statement would not immediately change the dollar's dominant downtrend, given the ballooning U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said the G7 must tackle imbalances from a long-term perspective - looking at such issues as growth potential and productivity growth - and should not rely solely on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates for a short-term fix.  \par \par \uc2 "The important thing is that this is not something that can be politically addressed with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates alone," Fukui told reporters as he arrived for the meeting with counterparts from the United States, Germany, Britain, France, Canada and Italy.  \par \par \uc2 Fukui sidestepped the question of how \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would defend its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which - along with China's tight \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar - has been criticized as forcing the euro to carry an undue part of the burden of correcting global trade imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "What we, Japan, want to say is that east Asia as a whole has been creating a new growth area. It had been led by supply-side growth, but the domestic markets are growing into big consumer markets," Fukui said. "This will be an important factor in correcting global imbalances."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts were not surprised at Japanese officials' defense of their policy. But some saw Fukui's observation about the need to correct imbalances as a step forward.  \par \par \uc2 "What is interesting there is ... that he concedes that maybe there need to be other types of measures to reduce the U.S. current account deficit," said Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with ABN Amro in Chicago.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040207e027001p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
