\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar gains on US jobs hopes ahead of G7 talks.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2004\par 13:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 6 (Reuters) - The dollar hiand gained versus the euro on Friday as upbeat comments from a Federal Reserve official raised hopes for robust U.S. jobs data, but trade was nervous before a G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Fed board governor Ben Bernanke said on Thursday deflation fears had receded "very substantially" and that he was "pretty confident" the U.S. would see big numbers on jobs growth fairly soon.  \par \par \uc2 His comments erased talk that the employment report at 1330 GMT would show little improvement after Thursday's unexpected rise in weekly jobless claims, and calmed the dollar's nerves before the meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors which begins later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 "Bernanke said what everyone had in the back of their mind: sooner or later we will see a big number in the jobs report. There is the upside risk for the employment report and if we do get good figures it will shift forward debate about a rate hike and loosen carry rationale to sell dollars," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HBOS Treasury Services.  \par \par \uc2 "I can't see how the G7 will put the dollar into a tailspin. After the G7, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will still buy dollars, Europeans will verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. won't be so bothered."  \par \par \uc2 At 1230 GMT, the dollar was trading at $1.2513 per euro , compared with $1.2539 in late New York, having hit a one-week low of $1.2636 in the previous session. Against the yen it rose to a one-week high of 106.69 , more than a yen above this week's three-year low.  \par \par \uc2 German industrial output beat expectations with a 0.6 percent rise in December, boosted by an increase in production of consumer goods and construction, in a fresh sign Europe's largest economy is recovering.  \par \par \uc2 JOBS AND G7  \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters expect 150,000 jobs were added to U.S. payrolls in January. This compares with a paltry 1,000 gain in December but many expect this number will be revised higher.  \par \par \uc2 The market is pricing in the chance the Fed will start raising rates in the middle of the year. The prospect of narrowing interest rate differentials between the United States and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as positive for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback has been under pressure on the view that G7 meeting in Florida will do little to halt its slide.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone policy makers worry that euro strength could derail an export-led economic recovery. Washington has so far been content to see the dollar weaken as this helps stave off disinflation and reduce the current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , authorities have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in record amounts over the past year to ensure the yen does not appreciate too fast against the dollar and choke off an export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "I see no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  slowing down (its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), and this is an interesting contrast from September," said Simon Derrick, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York in London.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow and Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki will hold a bilateral meeting at 0230 GMT on Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 Shortly before his departure for the meeting, Tanigaki 