\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  a stabiliser for market.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2004\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 6 (Reuters) - What's all the fuss about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and flexible exchange rates?  \par \par \uc2 Europe shouts for flexibility, especially in Asia. The United States pays lip service to the idea. Meanwhile \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and other Asian nations, continue to manipulate markets with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's case it is on a massive scale but also represents one of the few things the market can count on.  \par \par \uc2 As finance ministers and central bankers sit down in Florida on Friday for a two-day meeting of the Group of Seven, exchange rates and the dollar's precipitous decline occupy a conspicuous place in the debate as financial market volatility increases.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, investment measures of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility that look ahead one week are at their highest levels since the previous G7 meeting last September in Dubai, where a call for flexible exchange rates sparked a dollar sell-off.  \par \par \uc2 "Basically everyone is panicking about the G7, that's why they are paying a huge premium in the volatility market. They are pricing in a lot of turbulence over the next week through the G7," said Jake Moore, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Bank in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 But amid the chaotic market action the one constant traders have been able to count on is the dollar-buying/yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This is turning into something of a de facto stabiliser for financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen ($190 billion) in 2003 and a further seven trillion yen in January, providing perhaps one of the few elements of stability the markets can count on.  \par \par \uc2 Is there a limit to how long Tokyo can continue to sell staggering amounts of yen to protect its fragile export sector?  \par \par \uc2 "They can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  indefinitely by an infinite amount. I don't think that is a widely understood fact, that when you have zero interest rates there are no economic constraints, only political constraints," said Richard Jerram, chief economist at ING Securities in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "The Japanese authorities recognise the political realities in terms of the U.S. apparently wanting to see the dollar move weaker and criticising too extreme an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance. So they are letting the yen grind gradually higher," he added.  \par \par \uc2 BENEFIT FOR \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Without the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the decline in the dollar might be more broad-based, putting a sharp pinch on Japan's export-led recovery and aggravating its stubborn deflation. U.S. Treasuries, $1 trillion of which are owned by foreign central banks, would most likely decline sharply if the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended.  \par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Europe's recovery might not be hampered as much by the stifling effect a surging euro has on its exports.  \par \par \uc2 "Unless there is something really strong from the G7 this week, something that says Asia must take more of the burden of the declining dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the dollar turns around, and that doesn't seem likely for at least the next three to six months," said Barclays' Moore.  \par \par \uc2 Moore said corporate Japajibge ratio, or ability neutralise a falling dollar, to around 80 percent for January-March.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have allowed corporate \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prepare for a weaker dollar," Moore said.  \par \par \uc2 The constant selling of dollars even as its value declines creates a loss for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's special \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account, but analysts say the benefits of owning higher-yielding U.S. Treasuries more than makes up for it.  \par \par \uc2 "If the yen appreciates further, some economists and the media say the MOF account will suffer losses. Historically through fiscal 2003 (ending in March), the account earned 26 trillion yen in (cumulative) interest income while the unrealised \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses amount to roughly eight trillion yen," said Toru Umemoto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 With Japanese officials saying as late as Friday they will tell G7 counterparts \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stay the course on its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets stable, investors can count on the dollar depreciating against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The consequence for the next three to six months is for investors to buy the high yielders, buy the euro, sterling, Aussie (against the dollar)," said Moore.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040206e0260016q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
