\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar bounces to 1-week high vs yen ahead of G7.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2004\par 08:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 6 (Reuters) - The dollar reached one-week highs against the yen on Friday as traders bought it back to reduce their exposure ahead of a Group of Seven meeting starting later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar spiked to 106.25 yen in hectic trade, continuing an advance sparked by upbeat comments on the U.S. economy by Federal Reserve Board Governor Ben Bernanke.  \par \par \uc2 His comments prompted short-covering by dollar bears who had sold it aggressively on the view that the G7 would not agree on any substantial move or statement to stem its long decline.  \par \par \uc2 "Everybody has been thinking the dollar will fall after the G7 meeting and everyone has been preparing their positions for that," said a trader at a European bank.  \par \par \uc2 "So now people have started to think there's a chance the dollar will not fall as much as expected."  \par \par \uc2 As of 0630 GMT, the dollar was around 106.15 yen up about 0.2 percent from late Thursday U.S. levels.  \par \par \uc2 "I think some market players were trying to hit stop-loss orders above 106 yen," said Masamichi Koike, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp.  \par \par \uc2 Spikes by the dollar on Friday morning in Tokyo raised speculation that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the market - an inevitable conclusion after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record seven trillion yen last month to stem the yen's export-damaging rise.  \par \par \uc2 But there was no clear consensus and some traders thought nervousness exaggerated price movements ahead of the G7 meeting on Friday and Saturday in Florida.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was steady to slightly easier, parked around $1.2545 down less than 0.1 percent from late Thursday U.S. levels, but down nearly one cent from its one-week high of around $1.2638 hit in New York.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. RATE OUTLOOK  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's rebound started late on Thursday after Bernanke, an influential voice within the Fed, said the U.S. economy should post gross domestic product growth of four percent or better in 2004 and the recovery should be more balanced, with employment growth starting to pick up.  \par \par \uc2 His upbeat comments raised expectations of interest rate rises in the United States - a boost for the low-yielding dollar - although he indicated that low inflation provided breathing room for the Fed to hold off on rate increases.  \par \par \uc2 "If the dollar is to rebound, that's probably when the U.S. starts to raise rates," said Kota Kimura, an assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "So some cautious people were starting to buy back the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 Bernanke's optimistic view on jobs also led some players to speculate that January U.S. non-farm payroll data due out at 1330 GMT on Friday could be even stronger than the market's fairly robust expectations for a rise of 150,000 jobs.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the dollar met heavy offers from Japanese exporters, who are desperate to get rid of dollars they earn overseas, amid a growing consensus that the United States, Europe and Japan will be unable to agree on the best course of action for halting the dollar's decline.  \par \par \uc2 European policy-makers have voiced concern that the euro's surge against the dollar is watering down the euro zone's nascent economic recovery while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen have insulated the Japanese economy from a similar impact to some extent.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Friday he would tell his G7 counterparts that Japan would take action against irregular movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported earlier that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in October-December without the help of other central banks and sold 5.8755 trillion yen ($55.55 billion) to stem the yen's rise against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=105.76 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040206e026000wy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
