\par\par \b \uc2 SCHEDULE-Asia Business News at 0500 GMT.   \b0\par\par\uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2004\par 06:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Editor: Richard Baum\par     Telephone: +65 6870 3857\par     All times GMT.\par     TOP STORIES:\par     TOKYO - Matsushita profits seen up on TV, DVD sales\par     SEOUL - LG Electronics reports loss on TV charges\par     TOKYO - Mitsubishi earnings climb on energy, metals HONG\par KONG - Bird flu won't be SARS re-run for markets\par     - - - -\par     COMPANIES\par     TOKYO - Matsushita Electric Industrial, the maker of\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2  Panasonic brand electronics, is expected to announce a    \par \par \uc2  quarterly profit on the back of brisk sales of plasma TVs, DVD    \par \par \uc2  recorders and mobile phones. (TECH-MATSUSHITA (UPDATE 2),    \par \par \uc2  expect by 0800, by Daisuke Wakabayashi, 600 words)    \par \par \uc2  SEOUL - LG Electronics, South Korea's top maker of home    \par \par \uc2  appliances, is set to report a quarterly loss despite record    \par \par \uc2  sales, as charges at its TV tubes unit overwhelm profits from    \par \par \uc2  flat screen televisions and cellphones. (TECH-KOREA-LG (UPDATE    \par \par \uc2  2), expect by 0800, by Kim Miyoung and Jean Yoon, 600 words)    \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Mitsubishi Corp, Japan's top trading house, reports    \par \par \uc2  a rise in third-quarter net profit, helped by strong    \par \par \uc2  performances at its energy and metals division.    \par \par \uc2  (RETAIL-JAPAN-MITSUBISHI (UPDATE 2), expect by 0730, by Yuko    \par \par \uc2  Inoue, 600 words)    \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Japan Airlines System Corp, Asia's biggest airline    \par \par \uc2  by revenue, is expected to report a net loss for the latest    \par \par \uc2  quarter after a rise in fuel costs offset a rebound in    \par \par \uc2  passenger traffic after SARS. (AIRLINES-JAPAN-JAL (UPDATE 2),    \par \par \uc2  expect by 0800, by David McMahon, 600 words)    \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Japanese semiconductor equipment maker Tokyo    \par \par \uc2  Electron is expected to return to the black in the latest    \par \par \uc2  quarter as its customers boost spending to meet growing demand    \par \par \uc2  for chips used in hot-selling digital goods.    \par \par \uc2  (TECH-TOKYOELECTRON (UPDATE 2), expect by 0900, by Nathan    \par \par \uc2  Layne, 600 words)    \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Yamaha Motor, the world's second-largest motorcycle    \par \par \uc2  maker, posts a rise in profits for April-December, riding high    \par \par \uc2  on strong motorcycle sales in Asia and a large special gain on    \par \par \uc2  its pension fund. (AUTOS-JAPAN-YAMAHA (UPDATE 2), expect by    \par \par \uc2  0800, by Edwina Gibbs, 400 words)    \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Mitsui Sumitomo Insurance, Japan's second-biggest    \par \par \uc2  non-life insurer, acquires a 24.9 percent stake in Ayudhya    \par \par \uc2  Insurance of Thailand for $43 million as it expands its    \par \par \uc2  presence around Asia in the face of a saturated home market.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     - - - -\par     MARKETS/ECONOMY\par     TOKYO - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says it sold 5.88 trillion yen on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in October-December, acting by itself to hold down\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the yen, as Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki reaffirms\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Tokyo's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy before leaving for a Group of Seven\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par meeting. (MARKETS-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (UPDATE 2), moved at 0218,\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par by Kazunori Takada, 700 words)\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SEOUL - South Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor expresses\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par uncertainty about when depressed domestic consumption will\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par begin to pick up, but says is confident that a recent spike in\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par inflation can be contained. (ECONOMY-KOREA (UPDATE 3), expect\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par by 0600, by Yoo Choonsik, 600 words)\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SINGAPORE - The beleaguered dollar recovers to a one-week\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par high against the yen and bonds fall on an upbeat economic\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par assessment from a Federal Reserve governor and jitters ahead of\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par a meeting of the world's top finance officials. (MARKETS-GLOBAL\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (WRAPUP 2), expect by 0700, by Richard Baum, 700 words) HONG\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par KONG - The rapid spread of bird flu across Asia may provide\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par regional investors with an excuse to take profits but is not\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par seen doing the kind of harm to the region's growth and\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par corporate balance sheets wrought by SARS in 2003.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (BIRDFLU-ASIA-MARKETS, moved at 0430, by Paul Caine, 600 words)\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par KUALA LUMPUR - Moody's Investors Service raises its outlook\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par for Malaysia's foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  credit rating to positive as\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief says the ringgit's value is not out of\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par line despite being fixed versus a weak dollar.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (ECONOMY-MALAYSIA (UPDATE 2), expect by 0630, by Simon\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Cameron-Moore, 600 words)\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG - More than a quarter of a million mainland\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chinese tourists, flush with cash and ready to spend, flocked\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to Hong Kong over the week of Chinese New Year. For an economy\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that has suffered a protracted downturn and watched its\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par manufacturing base steadily erode, the rise of the Chinese\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par tourist couldn't come at a better time.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (ECONOMY-HONGKONG-TOURISM (PICTURE), moving shortly, by Susan\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fenton, 800 words)\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - ~- --.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040206e026000n1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
