\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar crawls up to 1-week high vs yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2004\par 04:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 6 (Reuters) - The dollar crawled up to one-week highs against the yen on Friday as traders bought back the dollar to reduce their market exposure ahead of the Group of Seven top financial officials' meeting later in the day.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar spiked to 106.25 yen in hectic trade, continuing its advance sparked after Federal Reserve Board Governor Ben Bernanke made upbeat comments on the U.S. economy.   \par \par \uc2  His comments prompted dollar short-covering by dollar bears who had sold it aggressively on the view that G7 nations would not agree on any substantial move or statement to stem the dollar's long decline.   \par \par \uc2  "Everybody has been thinking the dollar will fall after the G7 meeting and everyone has been preparing their positions for that," said a trader at a European bank.   \par \par \uc2  "So now people started to think there is a chance the dollar will not fall as much as expected."   \par \par \uc2  As of 0245 GMT, the dollar was around 106.10 yen, up about 0.2 percent from late U.S. levels.   \par \par \uc2  Its rapid spikes on Friday morning raised speculation the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the market. But there was no clear consensus and some traders thought thin trading conditions and nervousness ahead of the critical G7 meeting exaggerated price movements.   \par \par \uc2  The euro was steady to slightly easier, parking around $1.2545, down less than 0.1 percent from late U.S. levels, but down nearly one cent from its one-week high of around $1.2638 hit in New York on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  US RATE OUTLOOK   \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rebound started late on Thursday after Bernanke, an influential voice within the Fed, said the U.S. economy should post gross domestic product growth of four percent or better in 2004 and the recovery should be more balanced, with employment growth starting to pick up.   \par \par \uc2  Bernanke's upbeat comments raised expectations of interest rate hikes in the United States - a boost for the the low-yielding dollar - although he indicated that low inflation provided breathing room for the Fed to hold off on rate hikes.   \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar is to rebound, that's probably when the U.S. starts to raise rates," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "So some sensitive people were starting to buy back the dollar."   \par \par \uc2  Bernanke's optimistic view on jobs also led some players to speculate that January U.S. non-farm payroll data due out at 1230 GMT on Friday could be even stronger than the market's fairly robust expectations for a rise of 150,000 jobs.   \par \par \uc2  Still, the dollar met heavy offers from Japanese exporters, who are desperate to get rid of dollars they earn overseas, amid a growing consensus that the United States, Europe and Japan would not be able to agree what is the best course of action for halting the dollar's decline.   \par \par \uc2  European policy-makers have voiced concern that the euro's surge against the dollar was watering down their zone's nascent economic recovery while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has insulated the Japanese economy from a similar impact to some extent.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Friday that he would tell his G7 counterparts that Japan would take action against irregular moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported earlier that in the October to December period last year it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the help of other central banks and spent 5.8755 trillion yen ($55.55 billion) to stem the yen's rise against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  ($1=105.76 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040206e026000eh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
