\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar mixed ahead of G7, Bernanke influence wanes.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2004\par 02:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 6 (Reuters) - The dollar was mixed on Friday, unable to hold all the gains seen after the positive outlook for the U.S. economy offered by Federal Reserve Board Governor Ben Bernanke, as operators braced for an upcoming G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar briefly spiked to a high of 106.19 yen in morning trade in Tokyo, prompting speculation the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. However, there was no clear consensus since thin trading conditions were exaggerating price movements and the yen quickly recovered.  \par \par \uc2 In early business the dollar had mostly traded around the late New York level of 105.82 yen but by 0153 GMT it was around 106.05.  \par \par \uc2 Bernanke, an influential voice within the Fed, said the U.S. economy should post gross domestic product (GDP) growth of four percent or better in 2004 and the recovery should be more balanced, with employment growth starting to pick up.  \par \par \uc2 Bernanke's upbeat comments raise expectations of interest rate hikes in the United States, which gave the dollar a boost on Thursday. But Bernanke indicated that low inflation in the United States provided breathing room for the Fed to hold off on rate hikes until the recovery was roaring ahead.  \par \par \uc2 Trading activity showed investors reverting to the well-worn pattern of dollar selling as investors prepared for the two-day Group of Seven (G7) meeting of finance ministers and central bankers that begins later on Friday in Florida.  \par \par \uc2 "Basically, the market is in a wait-and-see mode," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The greenbacked continued to struggle in Asia after backing off from overnight highs, facing heavy sell orders lined up above 106 yen, probably from exporters.  \par \par \uc2 The euro reclaimed ground on the dollar, trading around $1.2560, up from the late New York price of $1.2541, but below Thursday's one-week high of $1.2636  \par \par \uc2 There is a growing consensus in the investment community that the United States, Europe, and Japan will not be able to agree what is the best course of action for halting the precipitous, but lopsided, decline in the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 European policy makers have voiced concern that the euro's surge against the dollar was watering down its nascent economic recovery while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has insulated the Japanese economy from a similar impact to some extent.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Friday that he would tell his G7 counterparts that Japan would take action against irregular moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported earlier that in the October to December 2003 period it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the help of other central banks and spent 5.8755 trillion yen ($55.55 billion) to stem the yen's rise against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 With a growing expectation that there will be no real change in the language of the official G7 communique, some analysts believe next week's trading could bring dollar gains as speculative positions built up against the greenback are reversed.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we could be back up to 107 yen or 108 yen next week," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "Bernanke's comment raises expectations for a rate hike, while speculators are now expecting a repeat of the the G7 statement from Dubai, which means there is no negative surprise for the dollar. That huge yen long speculative position will be liquidated," he added.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040206e0260006e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
