\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan to tell G7 it will act on irregular  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada  \par \uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2004\par 03:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 6 (Reuters) - Hours before jetting off to a Group of Seven meeting in Florida, Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki vowed on Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would stay the course on its stated policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets stable.  \par \par \uc2 "It is natural for us to make that stand," Tanigaki said minutes after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  published details of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the three months to December.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry report showed that all of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  involved buying dollars for yen during the period, when it spent 5.8755 trillion yen ($55.55 billion) to stem the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Amid speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  regardless of the G7 meeting due to start later in the day, the dollar briefly jumped to a one-week high of 106.19 yen from around 105.70 yen.  \par \par \uc2 As of 0151 GMT, the dollar stood at 105.96/01 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders said the spike could have been due to buying by the Bank of Japan, others said it was triggered by options-related buying.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar's quick rise to the high looked as if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a senior Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader.  \par \par \uc2 "If that's true then it's quite an unusual thing for them to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  right before the G7 meeting. It shows \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strong determination in preventing the yen's rise."  \par \par \uc2 Japan is concerned that a strong yen could derail the country's export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 But despite the massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the ministry said was carried out without the help of other central banks, the yen rose about four percent against the dollar over the three months.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry said it stepped into the market on 26 days between October 1 and December 31, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  most heavily on December 10, when it spent 1.2838 trillion yen to buy dollars - the highest amount for a single day in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 HEAVY \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained about 10 percent against the dollar over the year even though \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a record 20 trillion yen in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to stem yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Toru Umemoto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley, said he thought the ministry was targeting certain levels for the dollar/yen rate, although these changed from time to time.  \par \par \uc2 "Perhaps they watch the Nikkei (stocks) reaction and if they don't think further appreciation of the yen will hurt the Nikkei significantly, they are able to lower the line in the sand, the the level for dollar/yen," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued to maintain a strong presence in the market this year, spending a monthly record 7.1545 trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January.  \par \par \uc2 Despite this, the dollar fell to a three-and-a-half year low of 105.22 yen on Tuesday on speculation that Japan may ease back on its efforts to hold down its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the G7 meet.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials could come under fire at the G7 meeting from their European counterparts, who are worried that the euro might be left to bear the brunt of the dollar's fall, which could hurt European exports, traders say.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell more than 15 percent against the euro last year. In comparison, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank 10 percent against the yen in 2003, falling to 107 yen from near 119 yen.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. officials have also complained about countries artificially weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which they say gives those countries an unfair trade advantage.  \par \par \uc2 "Judging from previous meetings, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues will be addressed as part of discussions about macroeconomic issues," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 On a planned meeting with U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, Tanigaki said, "Rather than discussing something specific, I hope we can have a frank exchange of opinions."  \par \par \uc2 Reflecting the heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ministry also said on Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official reserves totalled a record $741.246 billion at the end of January, up from $673.529 billion a month earlier.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=105.76 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040206e0260002f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
