\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Watch: Main G7 Goal Is Not To Rile Europeans On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,170 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2004\par 06:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takeshi Takeuchi \par  \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Japanese finance chiefs heading off to meet their Group of Seven counterparts this weekend know they can't hope to get the U.S. to agree on a call for the beleaguered dollar to rebound.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 So Tokyo is setting its sights on second-best - allowing the dollar to keep falling but at least getting a free hand to keep selling yen for dollars to slow the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.  \par \par \uc2 At the meeting Friday and Saturday of G7 finance ministers and central bankers, Japan will likely be spared its perennial tussle with the U.S. over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy but will be treading softly in dealing with the Europeans, according to officials familiar with planning for the gathering in Boca Raton, Fla.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the only avowed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Group of Seven - has the tacit approval of Washington to keep selling yen for dollars, these officials and market participants say. Tokyo's main aim, then, will be to mollify the Europeans, who are growing worried about the dollar's fall and annoyed that Japan's yen sales are aggravating the dollar's decline against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 After the G7 last met in September, the dollar tumbled as much as 12% against the euro (although the European unit has retreated from last month's record high around $1.29). European officials have increasingly expressed concern that the strong euro could derail the euro-zone's nascent recovery. Against the yen, the dollar has dropped about 9% to three-year lows. Around 0550 GMT, the greenback was at $1.2635 and Y105.53.  \par \par \uc2 Although U.S. officials routinely say they support a "strong" dollar, "the U.S. has been happy about conditions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said a person familiar with Japanese preparations for the G7. With Washington unready to seek a dollar rebound, Tokyo's choice is to "keep intact the status quo," this person told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 That means that on his maiden G7 voyage Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki "must take extra care not to fuel the complaints of the Europeans," who believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  make Europe unduly bear the brunt of the dollar's fall.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 groups the U.S., Japan, Germany, France, the U.K., Italy and Canada. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also attends the meetings.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par             U.S. Mum On \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold a record Y20 trillion, almost all for dollars, last year to keep the strong yen from wrecking Japan's export-reliant recovery. It then upped the pace to a whopping Y7 trillion in January as the dollar continued to skid.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But although U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow has long complained about Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "manipulation," don't expect any real opposition at the G7.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For one thing, several people said, the U.S. is pleased that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been accompanies by continued monetary easing by the Bank of Japan. Even though Japan's economy has grown for six quarters in a row and seems to be picking up speed, the BOJ last month surprised the markets by expanding its "quantitative easing" - keeping short-term rates at zero and flooding the banking system with ever increasing amounts of excess cash.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If Japan didn't jack up the money supply in tandem with its dollar/yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Treasury would regard them as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation," which it disapproves of, said a person familiar with Treasury thinking. But if the BOJ keeps loosening policy, "the Treasury guys will in principle let it pass."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ kept policy unchanged Thursday. But after last month's surprise move in the midst of massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , John Taylor, Treasury's top working-level G7 official, refrained from criticizing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and praised the monetary easing. The BOJ "is doing an excellent job," Taylor told Dow Jones Newswires two weeks ago. "I am very pleased they are taking this approach."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another reason the U.S. won't complain about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is that President George W. Bush is far more concerned about maintaining Japan's staunch support in Iraq.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Bush administration has no desire to bring up the issue of Japan's exchange-rate manipulation at the G7 meeting" said Edward Lincoln, a think-tank scholar and former U.S. government expert on Japan. Given Tokyo's support for last year's U.S.-led war and its domestically controversial dispatch of troops to help reconstruct Iraq, U.S. officials preparing for the G7 "are all being told, 'Don't say anything, don't annoy the Japanese, be very careful,"' Lincoln told Dow Jones Newswires this week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet another reason Washington can hardly complain about Japan's yen sales is that, because they entail massive purchases of U.S. Treasurys, they help plug the giant U.S. budget and current-account deficits and hold down U.S. interest rates. Japan's Treasurys holdings were an estimated $400 billion at year's end. Its net purchases from January through October totaled $104 billion, accounting for about half the net buying by non-U.S. investors, according to Japan's financial daily Nihon Keizai Shimbun.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The end result of our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is contributing to the U.S. economic growth, and the U.S government is aware of that," said one Japanese government official.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                   Realistic Goal \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In terms of the markets, too, Japan's most realistic G7 goal is to maintain the status quo and minimize European complaints.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen likely won't surge, as it did after the September meeting, if the G7 repeats the call it made then for "more flexibility" in exchange rates. That statement sent the dollar plunging Y6 in less than two weeks as the market read it to mean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would back off from its aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But Tokyo has since shown it will keep \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and the market has realized that the statement was aimed more at China and other Asian countries than at Japan, market players say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A repeat of the September statement "likely wouldn't trigger massive yen-buying this time," said Satoshi Tate, senior vice president of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at UFJ Bank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, Tate said three of five customers and economists it surveyed expect the dollar to keep falling, barring a major change in the G7 script. But, said Minoru Shioiri, senior manager of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Mitsubishi securities, "few market players nowadays expect the dollar to fall below the Y100-mark in the near term."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That means Japan's Finance Ministry can likely limit the damage to the economy, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders say. Most Japanese exporters have set their planned dollar rate at Y105 for the six months from April, so their profits won't be too badly hurt as long as the ministry can keep the yen's rise moderate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takeshi Takeuchi, Dow Jones Newswires; 1-202-862-9285; Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by William Mallard \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 1187 4256  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040205e0250004y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
