\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Up Vs Euro, Yen On Position Adjustments  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 818 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2004\par 06:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar advanced slightly against the euro and yen on position adjustments in subdued Asian trade Thursday as most players stayed on the sidelines ahead of the much-awaited G7 meeting and other key events.  \par \par \uc2 Later today the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of England will hold monetary policy-setting meetings, with the ECB expected to leave rates unchanged and the BOE likely to raise rates by 0.25%.  \par \par \uc2 However, market players are mostly focussed on the two-day meeting starting tomorrow of Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers in Boca Raton, Florida.  \par \par \uc2 Europe may voice concern at the meeting about the weak dollar hurting its exporters and may unofficially scold \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. won't likely bend under pressure, said Junichi Kumagai, senior economist at NLI Research Institute.  \par \par \uc2 "Current conditions are, in a sense, best for the U.S.," as a slowly falling dollar supports its exporters, helps narrow its current account deficit and may gather votes for President George W. Bush in the November election, Kumagai said.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. probably doesn't find it necessary to change the status quo and what is likely to appear in the post-G7 statement is a repeat of the mantra for 'flexible rates,"' he added.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  also isn't expected to back down on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until its export-led recovery spills into the domestic sector, said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese Finance Ministry "will probably begin to reduce the amount of its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  only after the economy recovery permeates through the country," Fink said. "It won't succumb to foreign pressure."  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y105.54, above Y105.40 late Wednesday in New York. The dollar was at Y105.41 late Wednesday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2526, lower than $1.2538 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at $1.2535 in Tokyo Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y132.30, up from Y132.16 in New York late Wednesday. The euro was at Y132.13 in Tokyo late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.8306, down from $1.8311 in New York and lower than $1.8358 in Tokyo Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 For all the benefits from a falling dollar, the U.S. can't say its policy has shifted to a weak dollar, Fink said. "If the U.S. government publicly announces a weak-dollar policy, the greenback would likely nosedive" and may prompt foreigners to unload U.S. bonds, Fink said.  \par \par \uc2 And if long-term interest rates rise as a result, stocks may fall, the U.S. economy may slow, and its government will face larger interest payments on its growing budget deficit.  \par \par \uc2 That's one reason the U.S. at the G7 meeting won't likely harangue \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  about its dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is using to help finance the U.S. budget deficit by purchasing U.S. Treasurys, Fink said.  \par \par \uc2 Despite what seems to be U.S. tolerance, some players said Japan will likely stick to just slowing the dollar's fall against the yen for a while - action it calls "smoothing."  \par \par \uc2 Japanese exporters are said to have already sold most of their February and March receipts through forward agreements, NLI's Kumagai said. MOF officials probably know it and may not see the need of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the dollar sharply higher - especially before meeting their European peers - he said.  \par \par \uc2 Exporters didn't sell around Y105.44 Thursday, traders said, though some of them have reportedly set their in-house exchange rates near Y105.00.  \par \par \uc2 "Offers from exporters are thick above the current (dollar/yen) level, but they aren't coming down," said Tatsuro Karitani, vice president of Mizuho Corporate Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  division.  \par \par \uc2 Exporters appear active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options market instead, where demand for yen call/dollar put options has continued to rise before the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Demand for risk-reversal positions - where a hedger sells dollar call/yen put options and use the premiums to buy dollar put/yen call options - has been firm, as exporters and corporations increasingly see a weaker dollar ahead, options traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "Buying interest for yen calls show no signs of weakening at all," an options broker said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates that accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subscribers can find this table:  \f28