\par\par \b \uc2 NY FED: Dollar Fell In Q4 After G7 Call for Flexibility  \b0\par\par\uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2004\par 22:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell against a broad collection of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the fourth quarter of 2003, according to a report released Thursday by the Federal Reserve Bank of New York.  \par \par \uc2 The pace of the decline was varied. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined sharply following a call for "flexibility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by the Group of Seven leading industrial nations. The pace slowed during October and picked up again in November and through the rest of the year. The only break in the drop was a brief period in late October and early November.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. monetary authorities didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during the fourth quarter, the report stated. The New York Fed conducts such \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the U.S. Treasury Department.  \par \par \uc2 On a trade-weighted basis, the dollar's exchange value for the period declined 5.8% against the Fed's major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  index. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank 7.5% against the euro and 3.8% against the yen during the last three months of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Though data out of the U.S. supported claims of a U.S. economic recovery, concerns over the burgeoning current account deficit and the expectation that benchmark interest rates would remain at 45-year lows weighed on the dollar, the report said.  \par \par \uc2 The differential between the benchmark interest rate in the U.S. and rates in other parts of the world also dragged the dollar lower as investors reached for the higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the U.K., Europe, and Australia.  \par \par \uc2 Declines against the yen were tempered by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  publicly confirmed that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 reached record levels as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  tried to curb the yen's gains in hopes of spurring the fledgling Japanese economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of the fourth quarter, the current value of U.S. monetary system euro and yen holdings totaled $19.9 billion for the Federal Reserve's System Open Market Account and $19.9 billion for the for the Treasury's Exchange Stabilization Fund.  \par \par \uc2 A significant portion of the U.S. monetary authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is invested in European and Japanese securities. Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are also invested at the Bank for International Settlements in facilities at other official institutions.  \par \par \uc2 As of Dec. 31, the Federal Reserve and the Treasury's direct holdings of foreign government securities totaled $18.9 billion, split evenly between the two institutions. Foreign government securities held under repurchase agreements were also split evenly, for a total of $4.1 billion.  \par \par \uc2 -By Simona Covel; Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2371; simona.covel@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040205e0250003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
