\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Govt Bonds End Up; May Rise Further If Dlr Falls  \b0\par\par\uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2004\par 07:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese government bond prices rose for a fifth consecutive session Thursday, helped by lackluster stock prices and expectations that the yen will continue to appreciate against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Traders and strategists say bond yields will likely continue falling, particularly if the Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers don't make any new statements about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their meeting in Florida Friday and Saturday, and the dollar falls further.  \par \par \uc2 March 10-year futures ended 0.24 higher at 139.69 after touching an intra-day high of 139.77, the highest level for a front-month contract since last August.  \par \par \uc2 In the cash JGB market, the yield on two-year JGBs fell a half basis point to 0.060%, equaling a low in late January below which the yield hasn't fallen since last August. The yield on new 10-year JGBs fell two basis points to 1.260%, its lowest level since Dec. 1.  \par \par \uc2 "After the G7, if the yen appreciates against the dollar, especially below Y105, it's possible the 10-year JGB yield will touch 1.2%," said Kazuhiko Sano, chief strategist at Nikko Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 An appreciating yen boosts deflationary pressure and also forces some dealers to buy JGBs to hedge foreign-exchanged linked bonds.  \par \par \uc2 Helping the market to fresh highs today was pension fund buying of 10-year JGBs, according to a trader at a major Japanese bank. Short-covering by dealers also pushed prices higher, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Prices were little affected by the Bank of Japan's decision to keep monetary settings steady. Strategists say the short- and mid-term sectors of the JGB market remain strong due to the BOJ's easing of policy at the board previous meeting on Jan. 20. And some speculate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could ease further in coming months.  \par \par \uc2 In the JGB market, "the basic supply-demand conditions are tight - lots of investors weren't eager to buy bonds last year so now they are cash rich" and willing to buy, said Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities. "The circumstances are favorable for bonds - stocks are weak and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are dollar bearish."  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei 225 Stock Average ended 0.17% higher at 10464.60 after falling as low as 10374.90 earlier in the session.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Prices At Around 0600 GMT: \par  \par    Tiffe Dec 3-Mos Euroyen Price:    99.880  Up 0.005 \par    SGX Dec 3-Mos Euroyen Price:      99.875  Unchanged \par    TSE Mar 10-Yr JGB Future Price:   139.69  Up 0.24 \par    SGX Mar 10-Yr JGB Future Price:   139.70  Up 0.22 \par    10-Yr 1.3% JGB No. 257 Yield:     1.260%  Dn 0.020 \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mizuho's Ueno expects G7 officials won't alter their stance on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , disappointing any market participants that may have expected G7 to signal it isn't happy with the pace of the dollar's decline.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "So after the meeting, as usual, you will see a tug of war between market forces and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese authorities," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ueno expects \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to heavily defend any attempt to push the dollar below Y105, which "is an important psychological level for Japan's major firms."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After falling below 1.26%, the next big barrier for the 10-year yield is 1.2%, he added, which isn't likely to fall for two or three months.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But eventually Ueno expects evidence of a faltering in Japan's economic recovery will push yields even further, possibly below 1.0% in the latter half of 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For now, though, the economic assessment is bright.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan in its February report maintained for the third consecutive month its view that the economy is recovering gradually. But is also upgraded its view on exports, which it said are "increasing substantially."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, it held to its view that deflation will continue. On-year changes in Japan's consumer price index will remain flat for a while, but will then "continue falling slightly, since the imbalance between supply and demand in the economy still remains considerable despite its gradual improvement."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the swap market, the curve continued flattening out to the seven-year zone with major Japanese banks keen to continue boosting their fixed-rate exposure by receiving in the market. Still, there was greater paying interest in spread trading.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The five-year swap rate traded as low as 0.5575% vs 0.57125% yesterday while the 10-year rate ended at 1.2450% versus 1.26125%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In spread trading, the seven-10-year spread traded at 36.875 basis points.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the super-long sector, the curve continued to steepen, with the 10-20-year spread last quoted 0.75 basis points wider at 60.5 basis points.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Iain McDonald, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2941; iain.mcdonald@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Hiroshi Miwa contributed to this article).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1037 4153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040205e0250000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
