\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Ahead of G7, Japan shows resolve to hold down yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ritsuko Ando  \par \uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2004\par 10:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 5 (Reuters) - Japan's Finance Ministry said on Thursday it would tell a Group of Seven meeting this weekend that it would act against speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, showing it would not flinch at any criticism of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Toshihiko Fukui joined in, saying the yen's recent rise posed short-term risks, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept policy unchanged after a two-day meeting, after a surprise easing two weeks ago.  \par \par \uc2 "A strengthening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in itself is fine for us, but in the short term we must carefully watch its impact," Fukui said. "We must watch the impact on business sentiment."  \par \par \uc2 He also said the government's repeated yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not conflict with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary easing that was aimed at lifting the country out of deflation.  \par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry took an even stronger line.  \par \par \uc2 "We will state our usual stance that sudden moves in exchange rates are undesirable and have an impact on the Japanese economy and on the world economy. And therefore, when moves are excessively speculative, we will take firm action," Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi said.  \par \par \uc2 Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, also warned of further action.  \par \par \uc2 "We will take necessary measures if there are excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, with the aim of restoring medium-term stability," he told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Japan spent some 20 trillion yen ($190 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last year to curb the yen's rise against the dollar and another seven trillion yen last month alone.  \par \par \uc2 That policy could face criticism at a meeting of Group of Seven (G7) finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs in Florida on Friday and Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was trading around 105.50 to the dollar on Thursday after marking a three-year high of around 105.20 earlier in the week. It was around 116 yen per dollar before the last G7 meeting in September.  \par \par \uc2 BOJ POLICY UNCHANGED  \par \par \uc2 Despite the yen's surge, the BOJ decided not to loosen monetary policy after a two-day meeting that ended on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The nine-member board decided by unanimous vote to keep the current account deposits target, a benchmark of liquidity in the banking system, at 30-35 trillion yen ($285-332 billion).  \par \par \uc2 No change had been expected so soon after the previous easing, but markets had been wary of what the BOJ might do ahead of the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not a complete surprise, but there were some in the market who thought they could ease after the surprise move last month," said Chotaro Morita, a strategist at Deutsche Securities.  \par \par \uc2 The deposit target was raised at the last meeting from 27-32 trillion yen, surprising investors who had expected no change in view of the BOJ's assessment that the economy was on the mend.  \par \par \uc2 But the BOJ showed it was ready to fine-tune policy, announcing changes to its money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that include accepting floating-rate Japanese government bonds (JGBs) as collateral for market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  from March.  \par \par \uc2 That follows other technical moves by the BOJ aimed at ensuring that the massive liquidity it supplies flows more smoothly through the market.  \par \par \uc2 After the policy board decision, Fukui told reporters Japan's economy probably performed strongly in the last quarter of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 "I believe it is possible to see relatively strong numbers for October-December gross domestic product," Fukui said.  \par \par \uc2 The bank's monthly report issued after the board meeting described the economy as "recovering gradually" - the same wording used in its January report.  \par \par \uc2 Recent figures have shown Japan's economy ended 2003 on a high note, with unemployment falling and strong demand overseas for Japanese electronics and cars boosting output and company profits, pointing to an end to the country's 10-year slump.  \par \par \uc2 Economists have begun raising forecasts for growth, with early forecasts putting annualised growth at around 4-5 percent for October-December, well ahead of July-September's 1.4 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp, the world's second-largest car maker, added to the optimism on Thursday, reporting an 11 percent rise in operating profit in the October-December quarter, thanks to strong sales in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 But difficulties remain, particularly deflation and weak domestic consumption, and the rise in the yen is a worry for exporters, who are leading the economic recovery. (Additional reporting by Tamawa Kadoya, Chisa Fujioka and Jonathan Standing).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040205e025001bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
