\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar in tight ranges but fragile before G7.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2004\par 07:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 5 (Reuters) - The dollar hovered in a tight band against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday as investors kept to the sidelines ahead of a Group of Seven meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback held just above a recent three-and-a-half-year low on the yen, supported by commercial buying and chances of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the market lacked the energy to swallow heavy offers lined up above 105.70-105.80 yen.  \par \par \uc2 At 0605 GMT, the dollar stood at 105.51/54 yen up slightly from late New York's 105.41/49 and above this week's three-and-a-half-year low around 105.20.  \par \par \uc2 In the run-up to the meeting of G7 finance ministers and central bankers on Friday and Saturday in Florida the market has been roiled by comments, primarily from European policy-makers, expressing concern over the declining value of the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 This verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped apply the brakes to the dollar's slide. But the apparent nonchalance of the U.S. government, mindful in an election year of the pain a strong dollar exerts on U.S. exporters, has led to rising speculation that the all-important communique at the end of the G7 meeting will not present a united view on the dollar's fall.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is in a G7 waiting mode. No clear trend is expected until we confirm the outlook of the meeting," said Shogo Nagaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  section manager at Nomura Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears should continue to lend support at 105 yen, but a dip below that level will induce active stop-loss selling," Nagaya said.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was quoted at 132.21/29 yen compared with a late U.S. level of 132.10/24 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said Japanese exporters and investors were detected selling the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sporadically, locking in proceeds in euros into yen ahead of book closings at the end of the financial year on March 31.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady at $1.2531/35  \par \par \uc2 DOLLAR STILL VULNERABLE  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar was still vulnerable to speculative selling versus the yen, with major knockout options orders rumoured to be lined up at 104.75-104.85 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "There is still a possibility that the dollar could lose ground, considering option volatility levels remain high," said Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Royal Bank of Scotland.  \par \par \uc2 Implied option volatility in dollar/yen has been standing at multi-month highs.  \par \par \uc2 For example, one-month volatility was quoted at 11.00/11.25 percent, its highest since late October.  \par \par \uc2 In the shorter end, one-week volatility was around 14 percent, holding near the highs seen since last September shortly after the previous G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 In the latest Reuters survey of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts, the consensus view is for the yen to continue to strengthen despite record amounts of cash spent by Japan to curb its rise.  \par \par \uc2 Over the next 12 months, foreign investors' appetite for Japanese stocks, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows from exports and weakness in the dollar will probably push the yen towards the psychologically important level of 100 yen per greenback, a rate not seen since November 1995, analysts said in the monthly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll.  \par \par \uc2 "The weak trend in the dollar is expected to continue after the G7 meeting because no one expects much from the statement," said Hidehiko Inamura, a director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Citigroup in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "We feel there is a strong possibility that the market is going down below 100 yen in the next two months, but how soon depends on the the statement. If their description does not change, the move is gradual. If the Japanese government feels more pressure to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , then dollar/yen crumbles," he added.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Thursday that the U.S. economy remained strong and it was inappropriate for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to focus only on bad points such as the budget and trade deficits. The comments had little impact on the market.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also showed muted reaction after the Bank of Japan decided to keep its monetary policy unchanged after a two-day Policy Board meeting, an outcome widely expected by markets after a surprise easing just two weeks ago.  \par \par \uc2 Separately, in its monthly report for February the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stuck to its view that the Japanese economy is in a gradual recovery. (With additional reporting by Chikafumi Hodo).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040205e025000xm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
