\par\par \b \uc2 Europeans To Press G7 For Relief From Volatile  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkt  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Price  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 938 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2004\par 19:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--European officials are coming to a Group of Seven finance ministers meeting this weekend in Boca Raton, Fla., complaining about recent shifts in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - the state of which the U.S. thinks is just fine.  \par \par \uc2 The economic outlook has improved markedly since the group's September meeting in Dubai, especially for the U.S. and Asia. With Europe's economic recovery lagging that of the rest of the world, its leaders have been vocal in expressing concern that euro gains might cut off exports and cripple their recovery.  \par \par \uc2 At the close of the Dubai meeting, the ministers individually promised various economic reforms and together urged more "flexibility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to help make the global economy more balanced and sustainable. The message, primarily aimed at Asia with its heavily managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes, was interpreted by traders as a tacit approval for a weakening dollar. The statement helped weaken the dollar by more than 11% against the euro and a little over 7% against the yen. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the yen have moved very little in contrast.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet has referred to recent market developments as "excessive" and even "brutal."  \par \par \uc2 "In the present circumstances, we stress stability," Trichet said while taking part in a recent panel discussion in Davos. "We are concerned about excessive exchange rate moves."  \par \par \uc2 In contrast, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow has called the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment "orderly." An "orderly" decline in the U.S. dollar is just the prescription for a gentle rebalancing of global trade imbalances, say economists.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, while testifying before Congress, Snow stressed the benefits of flexible exchange rates alongside his standard line favoring a strong dollar, with the value set in open, competitive markets. The Europeans argue that they are suffering because of a lack of flexibility in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 "The European position is likely to be emphatic: spread the (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) adjustment," said Merrill Lynch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Yianos Kontopoulos.  \par \par \uc2 Edwin Truman, a former international policy advisor at the Federal Reserve and the U.S. Treasury, said the U.S. should resist any language aimed at preventing dollar declines needed to rebalance global trade.  \par \par \uc2 "The question is whether the administration is prepared to suggest they don't welcome any further dollar depreciation," said Truman, now a scholar at the Institute for International Economics. "My guess is they aren't and shouldn't be at this stage."  \par \par \uc2 Truman noted that past G7 statements have expressed a distaste for excessive volatility or have called for sharing the burden of growth. Ministers could link those sentiments together with the need to stimulate domestic demand in the context of U.S. external adjustment, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "The real risk is not that dollar adjustment continues, but either that its burden is not properly shared or that, in resisting it, not enough is done to stimulate demand," said Dominic Wilson, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Goldman Sachs.  \par \par \uc2 Failure to raise demand abroad forces more extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market shifts to correct imbalances. The increasing importance of countries outside the G7, like China, to achieving economic objectives underscores the view that the G7 is ill-suited to steer global policy, said Wilson.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. current account deficit, a broad measure of trade and financial flows, is running well above a 5% share of the economy. It is a level that many economists say is unsustainable and could lead to an abrupt drop in the value of the dollar and higher U.S. interest rates if foreigners lose their appetite for U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Under Secretary for International Affairs John Taylor earlier this week, downplayed that threat. He told reporters in briefings this week that the current account deficit reflects the fact that the U.S. is very attractive for investment and the way to bring it down is for other countries to grow faster and become more attractive. He said the structural reforms needed to raise economic growth would be the focus of the meetings.  \par \par \uc2 He also dismissed the relationship between the U.S. current account deficit and the large U.S. budget deficit, a frequent target of criticism from the G7.  \par \par \uc2 Japan, which comes to the table with best economic outlook in years, has long been urged to speed up restructuring of its banking system and make its economy more competitive.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. officials have praised the Bank of Japan for fighting deflation by expanding money supply targets, and have refrained from commenting directly on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy-handed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Europeans, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation has outpaced that of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may not be so understanding.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. is eager to ensure that haggling over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  language doesn't eclipse other issues on their agenda, including cooperation to combat money laundering and terrorism finance, and financial aid for Iraq and Afghanistan. A delegation from both of those countries are set to brief G7 ministers in a special session on Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 Developments in emerging markets and efforts to make the international financial system more stable are a regular topic of discussion. Conditions for the majority of developing countries have improved, with marked declines in borrowing premiums, especially for Turkey and Brazil. Several European countries and Japan are expected to protest how the International Monetary Fund is dealing with Argentina and that government's handling of its debt restructuring.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 includes the U.S., Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, and the U.K.  \par \par \uc2 -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040204e024000py\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
