\par\par \b \uc2 Gold To Track  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkt As Nervous Players Await G7  \b0\par\par\uc2 924 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2004\par 03:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Sinclair  \par \par \uc2 Sydney, Feb. 2 (Dow Jones) - With last week's brief speculation over Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bullion purchases having subsided, anxious gold traders and analysts now are focused squarely on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as they await a Group of Seven summit later this week.  \par \par \uc2 Most of those canvassed early this week by Dow Jones Newswires said they believe support for the yellow metal exists from about US$394-$395 a troy ounce and anticipate a trading range between now and Friday of US$395-$405.  \par \par \uc2 At 0201 GMT, spot gold was quoted at US$398.88/oz, down marginally from Tuesday's Comex close.  \par \par \uc2 But given the gold's overriding dependence of late on a weaker U.S. dollar, market participants say gold's medium-term direction is likely to hinge on statements and communiques coming out of the two-day meeting of G7 finance ministers and central bankers starting Friday in Florida.  \par \par \uc2 While officials are expected to discuss a range of global macroeconomic issues, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  players expect exchange rates to figure prominently in the deliberations.  \par \par \uc2 Of particular importance to gold will be any discussion of the U.S. dollar's standing against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Any sign that U.S. officials are content to let it weaken further, and that others are prepared to tolerate this, will undoubtedly be a boon for the yellow metal. Conversely, any suggestion that the dollar need not fall much further would be interpreted as a significant negative.  \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists say the outcome is unlikely to be as crystal clear as either of the above scenarios. Rather, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related discussions could very well focus on the thorny issue of distributing the burden of a softer U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Until now, the euro has borne the brunt of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's depreciation, with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in recent months to prop up the yen. And there is skepticism as to whether this can change in the near term.  \par \par \uc2 "It became clear when the Japanese released their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data for January that they have no intention of letting the yen appreciate markedly," HSBC's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists and economists said in a weekly report.  \par \par \uc2 "One could argue that if anything the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy has escalated since the last G7 meeting (in Dubai which called for flexible exchange rate regimes)," they said in the report. "It doesn't make sense to think they will suddenly agree to a burden sharing arrangement at the forthcoming G7 meeting."  \par \par \uc2 HSBC said if the dollar continues falling, "the euro will bear the brunt of it."  \par \par \uc2 Given recent trading patterns, this would clearly appease the gold market and likely trigger further gains. But what if the U.S. dollar is bottoming, whether on its own accord, or because Japan and perhaps even Europe don't want it to weaken much further?  \par \par \uc2 "We've raised this possibility a couple of times recently and are in the minority," Australia's Macquarie Bank acknowledged Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Where we tend to differ from others is that a fair portion of the market seems to think that G7 will come out with something tangible, and that what they say they want can be made to happen," the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  unit said. "We, however, have seen G7 train smashes too often not to expect another one."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par        Nervous Range Trading Expected Until Fri \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the U.S. dollar is in for a period of steady trading or even modest strength against its major counterparts, gold could easily see further losses. But given the range of possible G7 outcomes, and the various permutations accompanying each of these, it's not surprising that according to several traders, bullion could be in a holding pattern for much of this week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other things, Gordon Cheung, director of precious metals at Mitsui Bussan in Hong Kong, said the sharp bout of long liquidation by speculative funds that pushed gold down last week appears to have subsided. He is among those expecting bullion to track the euro point-by-point this week within a US$395-$405 band.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But others are slightly more nervous following last week's volatile trading.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Gold hasn't bounced conclusively yet in our view and hence we expect heightened volatility to continue with gold being even more sensitive than normal to changes in the EUR/USD," Barclays Capital precious metals analyst Kamal Naqvi said Monday in London.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 James Moore of TheBullionDesk added his concern that speculative players will look to book further profits on any near-term U.S. dollar strength.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Ultimately traders are waiting for the G7 meeting...for signs of a bigger shift in dollar/euro/yen trading sentiment, but I think this week we could see a little weakness creep into gold with the possibility of a test down to (US$385-$388/oz)," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The one event that could significantly affect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, and by extension bullion, prior to the G7 is Friday's U.S. non-farms payroll report for January.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The U.S. employment report will be crucial for the dollar, and the risks seem to be for less of an improvement than the market is discounting," said HSBC.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, weaker-than-expected jobs growth, as was the case in December, could trigger a U.S. dollar slide, and possibly help support gold heading into an interesting weekend in Florida.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Nicholas Sinclair, Dow Jones Newswires, 612-8235-2957; nicholas.sinclair@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040204e0240002p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
