\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Ex-BOJ Tamura: Policy Easing To Continue  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hiroshi Inoue  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2004\par 03:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan will likely continue to ease monetary policy to beat deflation and curb a stronger yen, according to former BOJ Executive Director Tatsuya Tamura.  \par \par \uc2 While he didn't speculate on timing of a further policy loosening, Tamura said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will boost liquidity by raising its account balance target.  \par \par \uc2 "The transmitted effects of a higher account balance target on the economy remains unclear," he told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. "But side-effects of the quantitative easing are still small, so the monetary easing will continue unless the side-effects become bigger."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nine-member board is due to start a two-day policy meeting at 0500 GMT. Tamura wouldn't comment on the likely result of this week's meeting.  \par \par \uc2 After easing policy at their previous meeting on Jan. 20, the board is widely expected to keep policy settings on hold.  \par \par \uc2 But market participants and people close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  say a further easing will be on the table at future meetings because deflation remains undefeated and the yen's continued appreciation threatens to hurt exports, which have been one of the main drivers of Japan's economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "Beating deflation remains a top priority, although the economy is recovering gradually," Tamura said.  \par \par \uc2 As for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, "possible steps the BOJ can take to curb a strong yen include leaving money stemming from yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market," which would push up the account balance, Tamura said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Dismisses Idea BOJ Eased To Avert G7 Criticism \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ has admitted that the yen's appreciation was a factor in the latest easing, but many say it was the prime reason for the policy loosening.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Y3 trillion rise in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's account balance target to a range of Y30 trillion to Y35 trillion gives the BOJ greater flexibility to assist the finance ministry's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop the yen rising.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a number of times in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets again Tuesday as the dollar fell to around Y105.25, its lowest level since September 2000.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the former central banker dismissed suggestions from other people close to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that the Jan. 20 easing was aimed at stemming criticism by Group of Seven finance officials this week of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 G7 finance ministers and central bankers will meet in Boca Raton, Florida on Friday and Saturday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tamura managed financial and payment system affairs at the BOJ before leaving the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 1996. He is now a president at Global Management Institute where he manages corporate governance and strategy. He has also been chairman of U.S. consulting firm AT Kearney's Japan office.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turning to Japan's domestic economy, Tamura said an asset price bubble, like that in the 1980s, won't return despite the BOJ continuing to boost liquidity in the financial system.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Japan may sit on dry firewood but there is no sign of fire right now," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tamura is skeptical that the recovery underway will be sustained.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The economy's recent recovery "isn't a real recovery because non-manufacturers and the services industry haven't improved, and land prices are still falling," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He added that problems within Japan's banking system won't be solved by a cyclical economic recovery alone.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Hiroshi Inoue, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-9203; hiroshi.inoue@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by John Gibson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040204e0240000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
