\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Expectations fade for G7 accord on dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ruth Pitchford  \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2004\par 17:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 4 (Reuters) - Financial markets are becoming increasingly doubtful that the Group of Seven industrial powers can agree on how to tackle dollar weakness and the latest comments of policymakers give little indication any such accord is likely.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists showed few expect the group's finance ministers and central bankers to say anything that will stop the euro and yen appreciating after their meeting in Flordia this weekend.  \par \par \uc2 The poll, conducted this week, showed 39 out of 46 strategists thought the dollar would weaken if the G7 stuck to the statement it issued after its summit in Dubai last September, when it urged flexibility in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Only 12 out of 47 thought the G7 was likely to agree alternative phrasing that could weaken the euro and yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's two-year-old retreat, fuelled by bulging U.S. current account and budget deficits, has hurt exporters in Japan and the euro zone, who are struggling to keep their prices competitive.  \par \par \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda, adviser to Japan's cabinet, said on Wednesday that Japan was likely to reiterate its concerns about the yen's rise at G7, adding: "I don't think the U.S. wants the dollar to fall further."  \par \par \uc2 But the U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said on Tuesday that the dollar's exchange rates were "best set in open, competitive markets," fuelling speculation that he will not support any G7 communique that questions the speed of euro and yen appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 In Europe on Wednesday, a French finance ministry source said France would press for structural reforms in the United States and Japan, not just the euro zone.  \par \par \uc2 But he was noticeably silent on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels ahead of the G7 meeting on Friday and Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 A European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  member was quoted as saying the euro's strength was having a big impact on exports but that its current levels were not unusual.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if she was afraid the euro's rally posed a risk to the budding euro zone recovery, Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell told Austria's weekly Industriemagazin: "Yes, I am...But the speed of the movements, volatility in other words, is a bigger problem (than the level)."  \par \par \uc2 ASIAN ABSENCE  \par \par \uc2 The euro has rallied by around 46 percent since early 2002, while the yen has strengthened by around 26 percent, with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  reining in its rally through intensive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 Some strategists say the G7 summit could put pressure on euro zone policy makers to cut interest rates, which are currently 2.0 percent against 1.0 percent in the United States, increasing the attraction of holding euros.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's fall was accelerated last September by the G7's communiqui from its summit in Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 Although many believe the G7's call then for flexibility in exchange rates was directed at Asian countries that peg their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders read it as lifting the threat of collective \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the dollar's decline.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists questioned in the Reuters poll expected the United States to find little common ground with its partners at the G7 talks.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is consolidating but likely to resume its broader downtrend," said Daniel Katzive at UBS in Stamford, Connecticut.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think the G7 meeting itself will have any material impact on the big picture," Katzive added, forecasting the euro at $1.40 in 12 months time.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday the euro was trading around $1.25, off its January high near $1.29. The yen was just above 105 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Reuters regular \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll showed 38 out of 50 strategists saw the euro at levels between $1.26 and $1.40 in six months time.  \par \par \uc2 A Canadian official said on Wednesday the G7 would discuss China, the biggest of several Asian economies whose exporters are benefiting from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  links to the weakening dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But strategists said the once-mighty financial grouping can achieve little to spread the pain of dollar weakness on its own.  \par \par \uc2 "The big shortcoming of the G7 meeting is that it doesn't include the economy that is pretty much the key to unlocking the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  conundrum and that's China. They're not there," said Klaus Baader at Lehman Brothers in London.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Douwe Miedema in Frankfurt, Paul Carrel in Paris and Toni Vorobyova in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040204e024002ni\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
