\par\par \b \uc2 UDPATE 1-INTERVIEW-Kuroda says G7 shares Japan's concern on yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima and Yuko Yoshikawa  \par \uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2004\par 09:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 4 (Reuters) - The impact of a strong yen on the Japanese economy could have significant global implications and the Group of Seven industrial countries share Japan's exchange rate concerns, a Japanese government adviser said on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda, special adviser to Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's cabinet, told Reuters in an interview that he expected Japan to reiterate its concerns about the yen's rise when G7 finance ministers meet in Florida later in the week.  \par \par \uc2 "It is truly amazing, in terms of both scale and speed, how the United States is propelling the global economy. Japan is almost half as big and it would be bad for the global economy if it were to slip back into recession and deflation worsened because of a higher yen," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no difference of opinion on this (in the G7)," said Kuroda, who as vice finance minister for international affairs until a year ago served as a "sherpa" to prepare G7 meetings.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think the United States wants the dollar to fall further, given it has already fallen more than 10 percent since September and even more over the last two years," he added.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to around 105.20 yen on Tuesday, its lowest in nearly 3-= years. It also hit record lows against the euro last month, skidding towards $1.29 to the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before clawing back up slightly. To beat back the buoyant yen, Japan spent more than 20 trillion yen ($190 billion) last year in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a further seven trillion yen last month.  \par \par \uc2 Asked why \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  so heavily, Kuroda said it was not so much the concern that exports would shrink but the likely impact on deflation and corporate earnings, given corporate capital spending was now a main driver of growth.  \par \par \uc2 "For Japan, the big task is to prevent a vicious spiral of deflation and a higher yen," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's core consumer price index rose from year-ago levels for the first time in 5-= years in October but economists say that was due to one-off factors and that deflationary pressures persisted.  \par \par \uc2 A higher yen weighs on prices initially by depressing import prices. Deflation in turn raises real interest rates - nominal rates minus the rate of inflation - and can strengthen the yen. Falling prices also make existing debt more expensive to pay back and thus dampen business investment.  \par \par \uc2 G7 STATEMENT  \par \par \uc2 On the whole, Kuroda said, the global economy had steadily improved since last year and the G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs were likely to confirm such an assessment at the Boca Raton meeting on Friday and Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 But Kuroda said there was no way of knowing what the G7 would say in its communique on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , declining to speculate on whether the Group would drop a controversial wording in its previous statement about greater exchange rate "flexibility".  \par \par \uc2 That language, introduced at the previous G7 meeting in Dubai in September, accelerated the rise in the euro and the yen, even though Japanese officials say it was referring to the more rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes of China and other emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Whatever the G7D (deputy finance ministers from the G7) discussed, they have no control over what the ministers might agree on," Kuroda said.  \par \par \uc2 Kuroda, now also an economics professor at Hitotsubashi University in Tokyo, said he expected the dollar to bottom out and turn higher by mid-year.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the dollar will reverse course at least by summer," he said, adding that in addition to the policymakers' desire for more stable exchange rates, financial market operators were too short in dollars and long in euros and yen.  \par \par \uc2 Considering the high economic growth, productivity gains and rate of return on investment in the United States relative to Japan and Europe, investors had to return to the dollar over time, he said. ($1=105.46 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040204e0240018z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
