\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar struggles on dwindling hopes of G7 help.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2004\par 08:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 4 (Reuters) - The dollar fell back near to a multi-year low against the yen on Wednesday on the fading prospect of any kind of support emerging from a Group of Seven (G7) finance ministers' meeting at the end of the week.  \par \par \uc2 Without G7 help, the dollar looks vulnerable, given renewed security fears after the discovery of toxic poison ricin in a U.S. Senate building plus long-standing structural problems such as the U.S. budget and current account deficits.  \par \par \uc2 However, the greenback continued to get some support from the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities, wimarket in 2003 to stem the yen's export-hurting rise and a record monthly amount in January.  \par \par \uc2 At 0630 GMT, the dollar was around 105.40 yen down slightly from Tuesday's late New York levels around 105.50 yen and just above its three-and-a-half year low of 105.22 yen hit in London trade on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese exporters were eager to sell the dollar, remembering how it had fallen sharply both before and after the previous G7 meeting in Dubai in September, when the finance ministers called for more flexibility in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation is growing that the G7 finance ministers and central bankers will not agree on any coordinated attempt to stop the dollar's fall at their meeting in Florida on Friday and Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 "They are unlikely to agree to stop the dollar's fall," said Koichi Ono, an economist at Daiwa Institute of Research.  \par \par \uc2 "The meaning of their call for "flexible" exchange will not be enough to change the dollar's two-year downtrend," he added.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 'S \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The call for "flexibility" in Dubai G7 was also taken as a criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sent the yen sharply higher.  \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  after that.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not clear that statement was aimed at Japan after all," said Fumihiko Kawano, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities. "No country has formally complained to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even after it did seven trillion yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, traders said the Japanese authorities appeared even more determined to block the dollar's fall this time.  \par \par \uc2 "Unlike the last G7 (in September), when the Japanese authorities refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the time of the meeting, their stance looks pretty different this time," Watanabe of Shinsei Bank said.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the Bank of Japan (BOJ) was believed to be keen to prevent the dollar from falling below 105 yen, where massive knock-out options are said to be lined up.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2545 slightly higher than a late U.S. level of $1.2535, having erased earlier small losses caused by a sharp fall in the Australian dollar after the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep monetary policy unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar briefly dropped more than half a U.S. cent from the day's high to around 76.10 U.S. cents after the Reserve Bank left its official cash rate unchanged at 5.25 percent. Although a majority of market players had expected no policy change, some had bet on a rate rise of 25 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 Later the Australian dollar pared its losses and traded around 76.40 U.S. cents.  \par \par \uc2 With the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting out of way, the market was shifting its attention to another high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the British pound, which has recently drawn support from the prospect of a credit tightening by the Bank of England (BoE).  \par \par \uc2 The BoE starts a two-day meeting on interest rates later in the day and is widely expected to raise rates by a quarter point for the second time in three month.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was quoted around $1.8380 near its late U.S. level of $1.8390.  \par \par \uc2 Apart from the BoE, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) and the Bank of Japan also hold policy meetings this week but no changes are expected. (Additional reporting by Chikafumi Hodo).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040204e0240010o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
