\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Weakens Against Major Rivals as BOJ Steps in  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Simona Covel  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2004\par 22:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar weakened against major rivals Tuesday on the growing conviction that members of the Group of Seven will be unable to reach a new consensus on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their weekend meeting, though apparent Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept it from slipping significantly against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Investors are discouraged by the mounting discord in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric among representatives of the G7 nations, who are scheduled to meet at the end of this week in Boca Raton, Fla. That has reinforced a sense that the U.S.-led call for more flexibility in exchange rates - first issued after last September's G7 meeting in Dubai - could well make a repeat appearance. That, in effect, would be tantamount to approval for further dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Already, conflicting statements from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials around the world have picked up pace and undermined the dollar's short-lived rally that followed last week's Federal Reserve meeting.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Ernst Welteke insisted that "a strong euro is an asset," in a speech in Frankfurt. That assertion led market participants to conclude that euro zone officials will not oppose dollar weakness at the G7 summit. In contrast, past comments from ECB president Jean-Claude Trichet on "brutal" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement convinced investors that the euro zone would take a harder line against euro strength, which has arguably hindered the tenuous economic recovery in Europe.  \par \par \uc2 "Now there's talk out of a European official basically praising the strong euro," said Larry Brickman, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rate strategist at Banc of America Securities in New York. "We don't get a consistent voice."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  further complicated the pre-G7 scene with apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to lift the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At least three rounds of dollar purchases by the BOJ were estimated to have been over $5 billion in total, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fought off the seemingly relentless dollar selling by the broader market.  \par \par \uc2 Implicit in that action is an assertion that Japanese officials will continue to usurp the dollar's fall against the yen as they seek to limit the fallout on Japan's export-led economy from a strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "They are making their view quite clear that the dollar/yen rate is important to them, and they're not going to sidestep around that," said a trader at a Japanese bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 In the U.S., Treasury Secretary John Snow reiterated the "strong dollar policy," though he tempered that by insisting that exchange rates are best set by markets. In language that was reminiscent of the last G7 meeting, Mr. Snow also praised what he called the "virtue" of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 The result of the resounding conflict is that the only thing skittish dollar investors are sure about in the final days prior to G7 is confusion. "It looks like more support for gridlock at G7," said Mr. Brickman.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 comprises the U.S., U.K., Canada, Italy, Japan, France and Germany.  \par \par \uc2 In late trading in New York, the euro was at $1.2531, up from $1.2427 in New York late Monday. The dollar was at 105.52 yen, virtually unchanged from 105.56 yen. The dollar was at 1.2490 Swiss francs, down from 1.2610 in New York on Monday, and sterling was at $1.8369 compared with $1.8191.  \par \par \uc2 Terrorism fears in the U.S. didn't help the dollar, either. The day started with the discovery of poisonous ricin in Senate Majority Leader Bill Frist's office in Washington. Several government buildings were closed as a result, and the scare sent ripples through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. "It renews fears in everyone of terrorism," said Mr. Brickman.  \par \par \uc2 As regards their influence on trading, the ricin reports were secondary to pre-G7 positioning in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and some analysts cited the poison scare as merely an excuse for already skittish investors to sell.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Australian dollar improved modestly ahead of a decision on interest rates by the Reserve Bank of Australia later Tuesday (early Wednesday in Sydney).  \par \par \uc2 The RBA, which is the first of several central banks to meet this week, is generally expected to keep the cash rate on hold at 5.25%. However, virtually all economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires expect rates to rise in the coming months.  \par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday in New York, the Australian dollar was trading around $0.7644, up from $0.7607 earlier Tuesday in Sydney and $0.7615 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 -By Simona Covel, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2371;  \par \par \uc2 simona.covel@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Tom Barkley in New York and Clyde Russell in Sydney contributed to this story.)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040203e0230001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
