\par\par \b \uc2 Foreign Central Banks Keeping Treasury Bears On The Hop  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Agnes T. Crane  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,047 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2004\par 18:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--It's funny how everything seems to come back to Asian central banks when it comes to the U.S. government bond market.  \par \par \uc2 Over the last month, the market has had a knack for rallying at the most inopportune times for investors insisting - and betting - that prices, in the face of a much stronger economy, should be moving down, not up.  \par \par \uc2 It's called the pain trade, a development in which the market moves in a way that causes the most losses to the most people. While economic data and a subtle shift in the Federal Reserve's monetary policy outlook have also hurt those wagering against the Treasury market, foreign central banks, most notably the Bank of Japan, are playing a big role in preventing yields from rising substantially. In fact, the strong presence of the Bank of Japan has even caused some domestic investors to doubt their own bets.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury market's reaction to the Institute for Supply Management's January manufacturing survey on Monday was a case in point. The headline business activity index reported solid growth in a sector battered by the economic slowdown two years ago. The component index sensitive to price increases, meanwhile, posted a multi-year high, which under more normal circumstance would punish Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 Prices initially moved lower, and yields higher, in line with how government bonds typically react to strong signals of economic activity. But the market quickly reversed course when the momentum petered out, pushing those jittery about their bearish positions to buy back some government securities.  \par \par \uc2 This so-called short-covering has been an important driver of intra-day trading since the beginning of the year as some investors, who were confident 2004 would be the year of the bond bear, have begun to question their faith in higher yields.  \par \par \uc2 The weak employment data for December and the Fed's hint last week of an interest rate increase sooner rather than later also caused many to rethink their views on the bond market, but big purchases by foreign central banks are acting as a crucial cap on Treasury yields, which move inversely to prices.  \par \par \uc2 "In general, domestic investors have been trying to play this from the short side, and their efforts have been frustrated in part by central banks," said Ward McCarthy of Stone & McCarthy, a forecasting firm based in Princeton, NJ.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Incredibly Deep Pockets \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, many of these investors, who take a longer term view of the market, have kept their positions intact, namely underweighting Treasurys. But Stone & McCarthy surveys of money manager portfolios have shown a moderation of what had been "extraordinarily" short positions - effectively bets on higher yields - at the beginning of 2004, according to McCarthy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the start of the year, a J.P. Morgan survey showed not one client polled reporting a net long position for the first time ever. But in its latest report, 7% of those polled had moved into long positions, while 39% continue to bet on higher Treasury yields - also indicating this pain trade has influenced trading positions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Apart from domestic data and the outlook on monetary policy, the Bank of Japan's record purchases of U.S. dollars have surprised many, since most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts believed the Japanese government would keep a lid on the amount spent in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, particularly in the lead up to the meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations, which kicks off on Friday. The other members of the elite club frown upon the manipulation of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  values.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, Japan has sold a record Y7.15 trillion in January, nearly a third of all yen sold in 2003 to buy dollars in an attempt to slow the appreciation of the yen and limit the damage to Japan's export-led economy. Many \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts believe the Bank of Japan is fighting to keep the dollar above Y105, which was evident from several forays into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets on Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And Japanese officials have indicated they don't intend to slow things down heading into the G7 meeting. Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki suggested during Tokyo trade Tuesday that the G7 would allow \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is a general agreement that (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) ought to reflect fundamentals and be stable," he said. "I think discussion (at the G7 meeting) would be along those lines," Tanigaki said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    If \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is any indication, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  believes \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be part of any policy mix designed to secure "stable" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   What's more, Japanese premier Junichiro Koizumi is expected to receive an additional Y21 trillion, equivalent to $200 billion, for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this fiscal year, which ends March 31. \par  \par    When Will It End? \par    \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants reckon the Bank of Japan has spent well over $5 billion over the last 24 hours in an effort to keep the dollar above Y105. That, in turn, has helped push yields to the lowest reaches of their current trading range.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government tends to recycle the bulk of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollars into U.S. government securities. Analysts believe these proceeds are behind the $46 billion surge in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings reported by the Federal Reserve in January.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Michael Cheah, portfolio manager at AIG SunAmerica Asset Management in Jersey City, NJ who manages $2 billion in fixed income assets, said Japan's purchases of U.S. government securities could easily top $550 billion by the end of 2004, with the only possible obstacle to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  being the remote chance of higher consumer prices in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of the end of November, Japan held over $500 billion in Treasurys, making it by far the largest single foreign holder of these securities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The logical limit will come when the yen creates inflationary pressure in Japan," he said. "The fact that money supply is not growing tells me that the inflation is far, far away. Although it looks difficult to believe, $550 billion, it is a possibility."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; 