\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Feb 3  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,064 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2004\par 10:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: In an apparent attempt to quash market speculation that its Group of Seven partners aren't pleased with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials in Tokyo said Tuesday the G7 shares the same view on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They also indicated that they won't back away from yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of a G7 meeting of finance ministers and central bankers Friday and Saturday. ("=WRAP:Japan Says G7 Agree On \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  But Market Not So Sure" ran at 0727 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA WATCH: After more than a decade building its stock market into the second largest in Asia, China is moving quickly to build a credible domestic debt market that is attracting domestic investors as well as foreigners. With around US$280 billion worth of Chinese treasurys outstanding at the end of 2003, the market's value is more than double the country`s holdings of U.S. Treasurys. ("CHINA WATCH: China Builds Up Tsy, Corporate-Bond Markets" ran at 0019 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian data showing a softening in business conditions and building approvals Tuesday added to the case for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold off raising interest rates - at least for the time being. ("=WRAP: Soft Australian Data Add To Case For Steady Rates" ran at 0423 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON: Japan's support of U.S. policy in Iraq will quell any criticism from Washington at a key finance meeting this week over Tokyo's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a prominent U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -watcher says. U.S. officials preparing for the meeting of finance ministers and central bankers in Boca Raton, Fla., "are all being told, 'Don't say anything, don't annoy the Japanese, be very careful,'" said Edward Lincoln, who was policy adviser to Ambassador Walter Mondale during Bill Clinton's presidency. ("=INTERVIEW: Japan Gets U.S. OK On \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  At G7 Thanks To Iraq" ran at 0403 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Economists at Standard Chartered Bank have predicted China will relax its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls in the second half of this year by widening the band in which the authorities allow the yuan to trade. ("=Standard Chartered Expects Wider Yuan Trading Band 2H 04" ran at 0614 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: The New Zealand dollar is unlikely to post gains against the U.S. dollar in the lead up to this weekend's G7 meeting, despite the unexpected rate hike by the Reserve Bank of New Zealand last week. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Rate Hike Doesn't Help Kiwi; G7 Meet Pressures" ran at 0459 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: The Indian government presented Tuesday an interim budget whose focus was suprisingly on fiscal rectitude rather than populist measures to win votes ahead of a general election. (WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Despite signs that inflation may be building, the Bank of Korea isn't likely to raise interest rates in February or anytime in the first half as long as domestic consumption remains weak. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary-policy committee meets Friday. ("=PREVIEW: Bank Of Korea Again Likely To Keep Rates Steady" ran at 0631 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's St. George Bank will conduct its first debt roadshow in mainland China this week and plans to tap into the country's growing investor base with a near-term Eurobond deal.("=INTERVIEW:Australia St George Targets China For Eurobond" ran at 0325 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Property developer Cheung Kong said it is offering HK$300 million worth of retail bonds in a second issue under its HK$10 billion multi-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retail bond program, which it established last year to diversify its funding sources. ("HK Cheung Kong Issues Second Retail Bond Worth HK$300 Mln" ran at 0301 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand retail bank ASB Bank doubled the original size of a U.S. dollar floating-rate note issue and achieved its lowest U.S. dollar coupon rate when it tapped the euromarkets for around NZ$150 million of funding in the last few days. ("=NZ ASB Bank Issues US$100M 1Y FRN At Sub-Libor Coupon" ran at 0125 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Manufacturers in Singapore are optimistic about business conditions in the first half of 2004 on the back of the economic recovery in the U.S., Europe and Japan, the Economic Development Board said Tuesday. ("Singapore Mfg Cos Upbeat On Business In 1H -Survey" ran at 0518 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia's trade surplus expanded to $2.41 billion in December from $2.16 billion a month earlier due to a pickup in exports, which rose a better-than-expected 7.6%, the government's central bureau of statistics said Tuesday. Separate data showed Indonesia's consumer prices in January rose a higher-than-expected 4.82% on an annual basis due to a jump in food costs. ("DATA SNAP:Indonesia Dec Trade Surplus Rises On Exports" ran at 0705 GMT; "DATA SNAP:Indonesia Jan CPI Up On Lunar New Year Spending" ran at 0713 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar faced heavy selling pressure against the yen in Asia Tuesday but held above its latest three-year low thanks to suspected buying by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Japan MOF Maintains Support For Besieged Dlr" ran at 0543 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES:  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Mitsubishi Motors shares soared Tuesday on a local report that the Mitsubishi corporate group and DaimlerChrysler plan to provide the ailing auto maker with a Y200 billion financial aid package. ("=WRAP: Mitsubishi Motors Shares Soar On Fincl Aid Report" ran at 00605 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Pioneer will buy NEC's plasma display panel \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the two Japanese electronics companies said Tuesday. The deal comes amid a rosy outlook for the overall PDP market, as demand grows sharply amid surging sales of flat-screen PDP televisions. ("Pioneer/NEC -2:Pioneer Eyes Top Global PDP Mkt Share FY05" ran at 0704 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: International fund managers continue to recommend being overweight equities in Asia ex-Japan, their favorite region for equity investment, despite stock markets being hit by worries over the bird-flu outbreak. ("=Asia Fund View: Funds Overweight Asia Despite Bird Blu" ran at 0652 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: Thailand remains a favored equity market in Asia despite the recent sell-off on bird-flu fears, said a fund manager with Aberdeen Asset Management. "We haven't changed our strategy...The market was due for a correction technically but fundamentally we're still bullish on Thailand," said Alan Kam, Aberdeen's head in Thailand. (=Asia Fund View will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: NEC Electronics will sell one of its microchip assembly and testing plants to Taiwan-based Advanced Semiconductor Engineering Inc. for an undisclosed price. ("NEC Electronics/ASE -2: Part Of Strategic Tie-Up Plan" ran at 0551 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: National Australia Bank's credit rating outlook will remain negative for now, despite the appointment of John Stewart as chief executive at Australia's biggest bank, Standard & Poor's Corp. said Tuesday. ("=INTERVIEW:S&P Waits For NAB Probe Before Ratings Review" ran at 0124 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Huawei Technologies has invested in a U.S. company making an exotic communications technology, a step that shows the rising global ambitions of China's leading maker of telecommunications equipment. ("=INTERVIEW: China's Huawei Turns US Venture Investor" ran at 0501 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Beijing Capital Land, a Hong Kong-listed property developer, said it will apply to issue yuan-denominated A shares on the Shanghai stock exchange, seeking to raise up to 3 billion yuan. ("Beijing Capital Land/A Shares -2: To Finance Developments" ran at 2351 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Airlines' management expects to conclude crucial wage negotiations with its pilots by the end of next month, Chief Executive Chew Choon Seng said Tuesday. SIA is under pressure from the government to cut wages and streamline \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to better compete against new budget airlines emerging in Asia. ("=Singapore Air/CEO -2: Launched Direct Los Angeles Flight" ran at 0739 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's Shenzhen Airport Co. said Tuesday it has signed a broad agreement to cooperate with Germany's Lufthansa Cargo AG on establishing a joint-venture air cargo services company. ("Shenzhen Airport, Lufthansa Cargo Sign Deal For Cargo JV" ran at 0304 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Hynix Semiconductor said Tuesday it plans to begin mass production of 512-megabit NAND flash memory chips this month, signaling its first step into the lucrative flash memory market. ("S Korea Hynix To Mass Produce Flash Memory Chips In Feb" ran at 0333 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Poultry firm Shanghai Dajiang (Group) Stock Co. Tuesday warned the bird flu outbreak in Asia could have a "big" effect on its business this year. ("Shanghai Dajiang Warns Bird Flu Could Hit Opers In 2004" ran at 0025 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Denso Tuesday revised up its sales and profit outlooks for the fiscal year to March 31, citing strong car sales worldwide. The Japanese maker of car electronic and electrical parts, affiliated with Toyota Motor, said it now expects to post a group net profit of Y102 billion for the full year, from the Y88 billion forecast it made in October. ("Denso -2: Raises FY Outlook On Strong Global Car Sales" ran at 0313 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Construction machinery maker Komatsu said its group net profit for the April-December period grew more than fivefold, boosted in part by strong sales of construction equipment in the fast-growing China market. ("Komatsu-2: Strong Sales In China, Cost Cuts Boost Profits" ran at 0640 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Major synthetic fiber producer Toray Industries reported strong profit gains for the October-December quarter, helped by improved sales and benefits from cost-cutting and other restructuring steps. ("Toray -2: Reforms Paying Off Better Results Than Expected" ran at 0638 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Casio Computer posted a group net profit of Y7.6 billion for the April-December period, larger than the profit it earned throughout last fiscal year. ("Casio -2: 9 Month Net Profit Exceeds Last FY Total" ran at 0637 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's largest brokerage firm Samsung Securities, an affiliate of Samsung Group, said its third- quarter net profit surged 70% on year to 33.6 billion won on cost cuts and trading gains. ("=WRAP: Lower Costs, New Pdts Buoy Samsung Securities Pft" ran at 0856 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: Philippine property group Ayala Land's net profit rose to PHP2.71 billion in 2003, up from PHP2.52 billion in 2002, while revenue grew to PHP14.62 billion from PHP12.21 billion. ("Philippines Ayala Land/Earnings -2: Higher Revenue" first take ran at 0821 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Cosmetics retailer Sa Sa expects its revenue for the second half of the financial year to grow, but expects beauty services to make a small loss, says Chief Financial Officer Guy Look. (INTERVIEW will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean auto makers and importers hope to lure customers back into their showrooms this year with an array of new models, after the market shrank drastically in 2003, industry participants said. ("=Car Sales In S Korea To Rebound In '04 On New Models" ran at 0443 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan Airlines System Corp. will soon offer new seats on domestic flights it hopes will help even Godzilla sleep like a baby. Japan's flagship carrier will spend several billions of yen to outfit its airplanes with more comfortable reclining seats, which a company spokesman describes as "a cradle." (story will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Carter Holt Harvey, New Zealand's biggest exporter of forest products, Tuesday said several of its manufacturing units would start negotiating greenhouse gas agreements with the government. ("NZ Carter's Units To Negotiate Greenhouse Deals With Govt" ran at 2211 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore-based commodities trading company Olam International plans to invest $11 million in the next three years to expand coffee and cocoa procurement and processing facilities in Asia and Africa, said Group Managing Director and Chief Executive Sunny Verghese. ("=INTERVIEW: Singapore's Olam To Expand Coffee, Cocoa Ops" ran at 0757 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \pa