\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Japan Gets U.S. OK On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  At G7 Thanks To Iraq  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takeshi Takeuchi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2004\par 05:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Japan's support of U.S. policy in Iraq will quell any criticism from Washington at a key finance meeting this week over Tokyo's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a prominent U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -watcher says.  \par \par \uc2 Ahead of the meeting of finance chiefs from the Group of Seven industrial powers, security policy is trumping economic considerations as President George W. Bush puts priority on his relationship with Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, said Edward Lincoln, a think-tank scholar and former U.S. government expert on Japan.  \par \par \uc2 "The Bush administration has no desire to bring up the issue of Japan's exchange-rate manipulation at the G7 meeting" Friday and Saturday, Lincoln told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview Monday.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. officials preparing for the meeting of finance ministers and central bankers in Boca Raton, Fla., "are all being told, 'Don't say anything, don't annoy the Japanese, be very careful,"' said Lincoln, who was policy adviser to Ambassador Walter Mondale during Bill Clinton's presidency.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, Lincoln said, the U.S. is happy with the dollar's general decline and unlikely to agree to a European push - shared to some extent by Japan - for the G7 to call for the dollar to stop falling.  \par \par \uc2 Republican administrations, such as Bush's, tend to preach open markets, and indeed, Treasury Secretary John Snow has criticized Japan for being a serial \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator. Japan sold a record Y20 trillion last year to support the dollar and stepped up the pace with an eye-popping Y7 trillion in yen sales last month to try to brake the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's climb to three-year highs.  \par \par \uc2 But given the administration's overriding concern with Iraq, U.S. finance officials will likely have to turn a blind eye toward Japan's activist \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy for now, Lincoln said. With postwar-Iraq policy becoming increasingly tricky, Bush treasures Koizumi's unflinching support, said Lincoln, a senior fellow at the Council on Foreign Relations.  \par \par \uc2 "This has everything to do with Iraq policy. What really matters is that Prime Minister Koizumi, like Tony Blair of Britain, has consistently supported President Bush," Lincoln said. "The Bush administration does not want anything else, such as the dollar/yen issue, to come up in the U.S.-Japan relationship that might create some tension."  \par \par \uc2 Not only did Koizumi provide key G7 and Asian backing for last year's war, now he is making history and stirring intense controversy at home by sending Japanese troops to Iraq to help with reconstruction. At huge political risk, Koizumi is stretching the interpretation of Japan's pacifist constitution to put the nation's soldiers near a combat zone for the first time since World War II.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par             Bush Doesn't Feel ECB's Pain \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. view toward Japan is in marked contrast with its approach to the Europeans, who have become increasingly alarmed at the euro's climb against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Over the past 12 months, the dollar has fallen 14% against the euro and 12% versus the yen. European politicians and European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials have recently become vocal in complaining about the dollar's decline, as the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  barreled to record highs, hurting euro-zone manufacturers. ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet complained last months about "brutal" exchange-rate moves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G7 comprises the U.S., Japan, Germany, France, the U.K., Italy and Canada. The ECB also takes part.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bush administration, while giving \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as free pass on its dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for, "is happy with a somewhat weaker dollar," Lincoln said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, Snow and other high-ranking U.S. officials continue to say they support a "strong" dollar. But over the past year, Lincoln said, "There has been a shift in the choice of words by the administration officials that suggests they are comfortable with a somewhat weaker dollar," Lincoln said. For example, the regular U.S. calls for exchange rates to be set by markets indicate they're happy with where the markets are taking the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The administration's preference for a weaker dollar stems totally from Bush's strategy to get re-elected in November, Lincoln said. A weaker dollar "would at least help create jobs in the manufacturing sectors and deflect the criticism of a 'jobless recovery"' made by Democratic hopefuls now competing to be their party's nominee to take on Bush in the fall, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takeshi Takeuchi, Dow Jones Newswires; 1-202-862-9285; Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com;  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by William Mallard  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040203e02300038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
