\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Biggest Test For G7 Is To Paper The Cracks  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,010 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2004\par 21:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Divided they stand, united they fall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 That appears to sum up the views of the different participants at this week's meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations in Boca Raton, Fla. They face the unenviable task of coming up with a policy statement that masks the divergent views within the group on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Nobody wants to implement any solutions and instead, this G-7 is a 'blame the other G6' summit," said John Vail, senior global strategist at Mizuho Securities in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 The differences in opinion lie mainly between the U.S., euro-zone nations and Japan and they suggest building a consensus at the meeting could prove very difficult.  \par \par \uc2 Broadly speaking, their positions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are as follows:  \par \par \uc2 -The U.S. wants exchange rates to be determined by market forces, just like the last G7 communique from Dubai in September advocated, a point reaffirmed Tuesday by Treasury Secretary John Snow, and is reluctant to embrace any form of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Instead, it believes strategies aimed at pursuing strong economic growth are the best way to overcome most problems. As far as it's concerned - the "strong dollar" mantra notwithstanding - the U.S. position on the dollar's broad-based weakness is one of benign neglect, as long as it helps address external imbalances and doesn't rock U.S. financial markets.  \par \par \uc2 -Japan, with interest rates effectively at zero and an economy long blighted by deflation, is a strong and visible presence in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly sells yen for dollars via agent banks to smooth out what it considers excess exchange rate volatility.  \par \par \uc2 Helping to keep the yen at a competitive level for Japanese exporters is one of the few policy options available to help reflate the Japanese economy, it argues. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year amounted to a record Y20.05 trillion and the authorities have lost none of their resolve, selling Y7.16 trillion last month alone.  \par \par \uc2 -Europe also wants stability (like Japan) and a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (like the U.S.) but will surely be pressing for stronger language in the G7 communique aimed at either curbing the dollar's overall depreciation or getting Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to share more of the burden of the dollar's decline. The euro's strength could eat into the region's exports and slow its economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Could Cut Rates \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone officials at the meetings, represented by Germany, France and Italy, may argue they've respected the last G7 call for "flexibility" in exchange rates and urge that Japan and other Asian countries do so too. That said, analysts reckon there's little indication the U.S. will give them much backing. Nor is there any indication euro-zone officials are prepared to act directly, either by cutting interest rates or selling euros, to weaken the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Canada and the U.K., the other two G7 members, have also seen their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit multiyear highs against the dollar in recent weeks but have barely protested. Canada, for instance, has quietly gone about cutting interest rates, something many round the G7 table might urge the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to do in order to stall the euro's ascent and at the same time boost growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Going into this G7 meeting, the gaps between the globe's big three economic areas look unbridgeable, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists reckon, no matter how wide the smiles are for the cameras on Saturday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This would suggest the wording of the communique will be just enough to paper over the cracks, perhaps making vague and standard references to "monitoring" exchange rate markets but unlikely to mark a policy shift from the last statement. Some observers expect the call for "more flexibility" in exchange rates, which should ideally be set by "market-based mechanisms," - which first appeared in the Dubai statement - to see a repeat outing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's not much room for a statement that has concrete implications for what G7's actions will be," said Steven Englander, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for North America at Barclays Capital in New York. Such an outcome will be "broadly dollar-negative," Englander believes.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 John Taylor, U.S. Under Secretary for International Affairs, Monday underlined where U.S. priorities lie when he said discussions in Boca Raton will likely include \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but center on raising economic growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexed its muscles at least three times in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets Tuesday buying an estimated $5 billion, according to dealers, reflecting just how committed it is to preventing the yen from strengthening too fast.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Strong Euro Helps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also on Tuesday, Ernst Welteke, Bundesbank President and ECB governing council member appeared to pour cold water on hopes of a rate cut soon, when he said a strong euro is an "asset" that helps keep interest rates low.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So given the entrenched positions of the U.S. and Japan and lack of European appetite for unilateral action, it could be a case of "more of the same" from G7 this weekend. And as Englander at Barclays Capital noted, that suggests the dollar will resume its weakening trend.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think they'll say anything that will be constructive for the dollar," agreed the senior dealer at a U.S. bank in New York. "The U.S. doesn't want it, doesn't need it and Europe doesn't feel that strongly about it."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's decline accelerated in the three months after the G7 September statement. But this weekend's communique is unlikely to contain a surprise element, such as the "flexibility" wording, so any ensuing dollar weakness probably won't be as extreme, analysts say.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.) \par    -By Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040203e02300030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
