\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: Plaza, Then Louvre, Now Boca? Not  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,351 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2004\par 17:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 G7 does not often produce major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  accords, but when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market has deeply overshot and the dollar is at an extreme, accords can happen. In 1985, the Plaza Accord sought to engineer a weaker dollar. In 1987, the Louvre Accord sought to stabilize the dollar (arresting the decline started by the Plaza accord). In 2004, with a major dollar trade weighted index at eight-year low, perhaps it is reasonable to look for the Boca Accord.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 But Boca does not have the same ring as Louvre or Plaza. Recall a G7 meeting in Garden City, N.Y., in the 90's ... can you imagine the Garden City Accord? The Boca Accord? Not. Boca is best known for early-bird specials, not accords.  \par \par \uc2 More seriously, the reason why Boca will not end in an accord to stabilize the dollar has much more to do with the Bush administration's embrace of an orderly dollar decline than the ring of Boca Raton or the English translation: rat's mouth. For the Bush team, a weak dollar is just what the doctor ordered to help grow jobs and restore U.S. manufacturing to a growth path. Ultimately, a weaker dollar will help drive business investment. And the dollar's decline has been orderly. The U.S. bond market has not suffered from any shortfall of foreign investment ... surely not with Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaking up billions monthly in the bond market as it recycles its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars. And U.S. stocks have been helped by the dollar slide to date mainly through the translation effect (firms report higher earnings when foreign income is converted into dollars).  \par \par \uc2 So any hope of a Boca Accord rests with the case that European and Japanese officials make for stabilizing the dollar. Surely no U.S. official is eager to see the dollar become unstable. But Europe's and Japan's definition of unstable is not shared by the U.S. Indeed, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns in Europe and Japan do not hold up under closer scrutiny. European fundamentals are improving, and exports have held up despite the rise in the euro; Japanese exporters are reporting solid earnings, not just sales; and U.S. demand may be trumping the effect of a weak dollar when it comes to European and Japanese competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 Ruling out a Boca Accord, however, leaves scope for some lesser agreements, like burden sharing that Europe is keen on achieving at G7. What is burden sharing? Simply that Asia share in the burden of adjustment associated with the record U.S. current account deficit that Europe arguably has largely born in the last two years. Indeed, this was the intent of the Dubai G7 communique which called for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility. But at Dubai, there was no specific mention of Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes, and markets took the statement literally as an endorsement to freely sell dollars across the board. And since Dubai, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hit record levels (rewriting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a developed nation.)  \par \par \uc2 Why can't Europe get the U.S. to force Japan to share the burden of adjustment more equally? Or, in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms, why won't Europe and the U.S. get Japan to let the yen appreciate at a pace set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market? Because the U.S. and Japan have gotten particularly cozy on a geopolitical plane since Prime Minister Koizumi agreed to send Japanese troops to Iraq, risking his political career (first time Japan has put troops in harm's way since World War II, and some argue it violates Japan's constitution). And, U.S. Treasury is keenly aware that Japan is buying U.S. Treasurys with the dollars it accumulates from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... a large amount of U.S. Treasurys. Indeed, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone has been accumulating in the neighborhood of 30%-40% of the Treasury debt auctions as well as regularly buying Treasury notes between auctions. In other words, the official demand for U.S. debt out of Japan is keeping U.S. interest rates (read mortgage rates) lower than they would be otherwise. Moreover, dollar/yen is down from Y112, or about 6% since the Dubai meeting, indicating Japan is bearing some of the load for adjustment (though euro/yen is 3% higher). And make no mistake, U.S. Under Secretary for International Affairs John Taylor on Monday praised Japan for easing monetary policy (presumably a reference to the BoJ move in January). As far as the U.S. is concerned, Japan is pulling its load for domestic and global growth.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, I sense that the U.S. (and G7 generally) has been given assurances by China that the yuan peg will be loosened gradually and the first step will come later this year. So the flexibility call/condition at the Dubai meeting has largely been met as far as the U.S. is concerned.  \par \par \uc2 How can \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  escape G7 criticism for unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? It probably will not pass the European smell test, but for the U.S. between the bond buying it has generated and the 6% move down in dollar/yen since Dubai, the "smoothing" \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not a problem. And this is not lost on MoF, which is going to lengths to make its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appear to be smoothing as opposed to targeting or fixing ... dollar/yen is "floating" gradually lower. I also believe MoF is keen to keep dollar/yen above Y105 ahead of G7, aware of the significant option-related sell orders if it breaks by Friday. It follows that dollar/yen will be allowed to move under Y105 after G7, albeit gradually. Japan will continue its "smoothing" \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  into and after G7.  \par \par \uc2 Lastly, I doubt G7 will change the language of the communique much, if at all, from the Dubai statement when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 . First, by doing so, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility might rise ... markets would assume G7 is deliberately attempting to steer the dollar. Second, there is Taylor's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rule. In principle, Taylor, and the Bush administration in general, opposes attempts by G7 to influence market mechanisms and especially manipulating \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. Taylor and presumably Snow will resist any effort by Europe and Japan to rewrite the language of the (Dubai) communique to stabilize the dollar or even protest excess volatility.  \par \par \uc2 Taylor also made it very clear late Monday that G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  discussions will be routine, and not the center piece of the meeting. Indeed, Japan's Finance Minister Tanigaki echoed this view earlier today.  \par \par \uc2 Bottom line: I am betting on no change in the statement. Europe has the power to affect the exchange rate with a rate cut, which the U.S. would welcome (the rate cut). And calling for stable exchange rates or a sentence protesting excessive volatility smacks of manipulation, which Taylor finds repugnant. Could a line be added on excessive volatility? Perhaps if the ECB agreed to cut rates as its next move to address the euro overshoot. If it gets into the statement, assume the ECB has given a nod and wink on a future rate cut. Burden sharing has little chance of making into the statement for the reasons cited above (look for euro/yen to rally post G7 if not mentioned). Clearly an unchanged statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Saturday will prompt a wave of dollar selling. If excessive volatility makes it in, assume the ECB has agreed to a rate-cut option and suspect the dollar sees a decent bounce. The odds of excessive volatility making it in can be put at 30%. So, an unchanged \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement in the G7 communique Saturday is the sure bet.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.)  \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040203e02300023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
