\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-G7 members downplay chance of new dollar agreement.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Dolan  \par \uc2 922 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2004\par 18:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from Belgium's Reynders, IMF's Carstens, ECB's Welteke and European diplomatic source)  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 3 (Reuters) - Key members of the Group of Seven richest nations, whose finance ministers meet in Florida later this week, on Tuesday dampened speculation about any global agreement to arrest the U.S. dollar's steep decline.  \par \par \uc2 Financial markets, nervous for weeks the Boca Raton meeting on Friday and Saturday may agree words or action to buoy the dollar, now reckon lack of consensus will stymie any initiative.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, down almost nine percent against a basket of world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since G7 last met in Dubai in September, hit a three-year low against Japan's yen on Tuesday and dropped one percent on the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told a news conference in Tokyo earlier that the recovering world economy and structural issues underlying it would have more weight than \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues at the annual February gathering.  \par \par \uc2 "Macroeconomy, structural issues - these will have the most weight in the discussions," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's comments echoed U.S. Treasury Undersecretary John Taylor, who said on Monday that G7 commitments to lift economic growth in all regions would dominate the weekend gathering.  \par \par \uc2 "The agenda for growth, which is a very important initiative launched in Dubai back in September, will be the focus of the policy discussions at the G7 meetings," he said, declining even to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS TAKE THEIR CUE  \par \par \uc2 Renewed dollar weakness indicated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders believe no new agreement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates will emerge from this week's meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the United States, Japan, Germany, France, Britain, Italy and Canada.  \par \par \uc2 In the absence of that, rising U.S. trade and budget deficits will see the dollar resume its slide, they said.  \par \par \uc2 As long as the deficits continue to expand, the dollar needs more and more foreign investment into the United States and its assets just to keep its exchange rate steady.  \par \par \uc2 Economists reckon a cheaper dollar may help attract foreigners back to U.S. markets while easing the country's trade deficit by boosting U.S. exports and limiting imports.  \par \par \uc2 But official purchases of dollars by Asian governments in particular, aimed at keeping their exports competitive in U.S. markets, complicates the adjustment by preventing dollar losses in regions where the U.S. has the biggest trade imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 The official dollar purchases, banked in U.S. Treasury bonds, also artificially fund U.S. budget gaps and remove pressure on Washington to tighten fiscal policy.  \par \par \uc2 "People are beginning to realise there won't be much coming out of the G7 that will change the dollar's weak trend," said Marvin Barth, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London.  \par \par \uc2 EUROPE SEEKS SOMETHING  \par \par \uc2 The stance of the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was suspected by traders of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet again to buy dollars for yen on Tuesday, contrasts with European concerns that the euro is taking most of the heat from dollar depreciation.  \par \par \uc2 "I've always said that what worries me the most were \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shocks, a too rapid change in exchange rates," Belgian Finance Minister Didier Reynders said on Tuesday. "A euro at $1.30 seems to me a threshold to a rather critical level."  \par \par \uc2 The euro set a record high just shy of $1.29 last month. At the very least, euro zone delegations to G7 are expected to seek to add a phrase to the G7 communique that says excess volatility on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is unwelcome while adding a call for a greater sharing of the dollar decline.  \par \par \uc2 This is because the dollar's loss of more than 12 percent against the euro since September is the highest against all major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and European governments are concerned this could stifle the primarily export-led recovery in the euro zone.  \par \par \uc2 "At the G7, economic, fiscal and exchange rate policies will be discussed," a European diplomatic source said on Tuesday. "There's a sensation that it (the euro) is reaching elevated levels at which we need to start worrying."  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone governments have complained the Dubai G7 statement seeking "more flexibility in exchange rates" was wrongly interpreted by markets as a green light to sell dollars for euros because other countries actively supported the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Asian economic giants such as Japan, China, India and South Korea, to which the statement was addressed, either continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit dollar losses or fix their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. unit. A problem for G7 is neither China nor the other Asia economic powerhouses outside Japan are party to G7 agreements.  \par \par \uc2 "In terms of raising the issue and persuading China to react, I think the aim has been achieved," said a senior Japanese finance ministry official on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 In Frankfurt, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Ernst Welteke on Tuesday played down fears that a strong euro will hurt the euro zone economic recovery, which he said was well under way but not yet self-sustaining.  \par \par \uc2 "Currently, the strong euro allows us to keep interest rates at a low level in an environment where demand is picking up and excess liquidity persists," he said in prepared speech notes.  \par \par \uc2 International Monetary Fund Deputy Managing Director Augustin Carstens said in Dar Es Salaam on Tuesday there was no need to act now to stem the dollar's decline but the situation should be monitored closely.  \par \par \uc2 "At this stage, there is no need for panic."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040203e023002ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
