\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls across board on G7 concerns, ricin.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2004\par 12:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 3 (Reuters) - The dollar slid to a new three-year low against the yen on Tuesday and lost more than one percent on the euro after a U.S. official fanned the view G7 ministers would offer the greenback little help this week.  \par \par \uc2 News that the poison ricin was discovered in U.S. Senate mail also hurt the dollar, as the incident rekindled concerns about attacks in the United States which dogged the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after September 11, 2001.  \par \par \uc2 "Ricin is certainly one factor obviously because of renewed geopolitical issues. But a major factor is speculation that the G7 will not be able to stem the dollar's decline," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One in London.  \par \par \uc2 By 1024 GMT the euro had stormed one percent higher on the day to $1.2568 before settling around $1.2552. It hit a record peak just below $1.29 in January.  \par \par \uc2 Finance ministers from the Group of Seven richest nations meet in Florida on Friday and Saturday and the market is waiting to see if they address the dollar's broad two-year decline, although many doubt they have a common position.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Undersecretary John Taylor said late on Monday that while he expected some discussion on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as always, an agenda for growth launched at last September's G7 meeting would be the centrepiece of this week's gathering.  \par \par \uc2 "We expect no major revision in the G7 statement, so prefer to go dollar short now," said Trevor Dinmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Dinmore said the market was nervous the dollar could be sold off after the G7, as was the case after the September statement from Dubai. That statement called for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility, a call the market interpreted as urging Asian nations to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slid to 105.32 yen, its lowest in three years but only slightly down from Monday's closing levels.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback also fell one percent on the Swiss franc, Australian dollar and the British pound.  \par \par \uc2 CHIEF CONCERN  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been under pressure over concerns about the U.S. current account deficit and the perception Washington is comfortable with a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The White House released its economic forecasts on Monday and expected a budget deficit at $521 billion in fiscal year 2004, fuelling negative dollar sentiment as it reminded the market of another key driver of dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki echoed Taylor, saying on Tuesday that macroeconomy and structural issues would have most weight in the talks, although he added: "The issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be neglected by the G7."  \par \par \uc2 In the euro zone, only European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council member Ernst Welteke is scheduled to speak later on Tuesday, so the market may have to wait for the bank's policy meeting and news conference on Thursday for its latest position on euro strength. The euro has gained roughly 40 percent on the dollar in the past two years.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow testifies on the 2005 budget at 1500 GMT.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH  \par \par \uc2 While the dollar fell steeply against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, the threat of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to limit its losses against the yen.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent 7.15 trillion yen ($67.8 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in January - a record monthly figure, on top of an annual record of 20 trillion yen in 2003 - to stem the yen's rise. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said however the absence of a G7 warning against the dollar's decline could be taken as a green light to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a fall beyond 105 yen could trigger an even sharper decline, given the huge amount of option positions lined up there.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040203e02300263\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
