\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan seeks to deflate pre-G7  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  speculation.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2004\par 09:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 3 (Reuters) - Japan sought on Tuesday to lower expectations about any Group of Seven pact later this week to deal with the dollar's precipitous fall but insisted that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be discussed. Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said the meeting of his counterparts from the G7 leading economies in Boca Raton, Florida, on Friday and Saturday would focus on broad economic issues.  \par \par \uc2 "Macroeconomy, structural issues - these will have the most weight in the discussions," Tanigaki told a news conference.  \par \par \uc2 Still, with the dollar sinking to its lowest level against the yen in nearly 3-= years on Tuesday, threatening to dampen Japan's export-driven economic recovery, Tanigaki emphasised that the dollar's depreciation would not go unnoticed.  \par \par \uc2 "The issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be neglected by the G7," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is fixated on whether the G7 finance ministers and central bankers will try to halt the dollar's decline, perhaps by dropping from their communique a reference to greater exchange rate flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 That language, introduced at the previous G7 meeting in September in Dubai, had been perceived in the market as a green light to sell dollars more aggressively.  \par \par \uc2 A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official declined to say whether the G7 would amend the wording - if only to address the market's perception - saying the outcome hinged on how the talks between the finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs panned out.  \par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN CONCERNS  \par \par \uc2 Complaints from Europe about the euro's 20-percent rise over the past year to record highs against the dollar are, at least on the surface, similar to Japan's stance.  \par \par \uc2 But more recently the dollar has faced renewed selling against the yen as signs emerged that Europe was also unhappy about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, the MOF said it had conducted a monthly record of 7.15 trillion yen ($67.8 billion) of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January, on top of an annual record of 20 trillion yen in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Despite such huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar hit 105.32 yen on Tuesday, its lowest in around 3-= years.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF official said Japan and Europe shared concerns about the dollar's fall, although this did not mean they would be pitted against the United States at the Boca Raton meeting.  \par \par \uc2 "What has been going on is a decline in the dollar against all non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In that sense, Japan and Europe have a common interest," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if Japan and Europe would form a united front to persuade the United States to agree on halting the dollar's decline, he said such a juxtaposition was inappropriate.  \par \par \uc2 "This is not about antagonism. Japan, Europe and the United States cooperate as much as they can to address issues that each country may have."  \par \par \uc2 He also said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had the understanding of its G7 partners about its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given the vulnerability of its economy to a higher yen, due in part to the proportion of its trade conducted in non-yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Although Japan has one of the three main \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is relatively small, especially after the birth of the euro, and the impact from exchange rate fluctuation is large. The need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is high and the other countries understand that."  \par \par \uc2 On the question of whether China should loosen its grip on the yuan, which is pegged to the dollar, the MOF official said the G7 had already made a point with its Dubai statement.  \par \par \uc2 "In terms of raising the issue and persuading China to react, I think the aim has been achieved to a certain extent," he said, adding that emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were unlikely to be a major issue at the Boca Raton meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Zembei Mizoguchi, Japan's top financial diplomat, who met with other G7 sherpas in Brussels last week, said Japan's policy was in line with those of the rest of the G7.  \par \par \uc2 "Everyone is looking towards expanding their economies, and so it is important that G7 countries run their economies in an appropriate way," the vice finance minister for international affairs told reporters. ($1=105.53 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040203e0230015b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
