\par\par \b \uc2 Tanigaki seeks to deflect market's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  focus on G7.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2004\par 03:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 3 (Reuters) - Japan said on Tuesday that this week's Group of Seven meeting would focus on broader economic issues but sought to avoid giving the impression that the world's top economies had given up on the dollar's precipitous fall.   \par \par \uc2  Although financial markets have been fixated about whether the G7 finance ministers and central bankers would try to halt the dollar's decline, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said the talks would centre on how to achieve healthy global growth.   \par \par \uc2  "Macroeconomy, structural issues - these will have the most weight in the discussions," Tanigaki told a news conference.   \par \par \uc2  The remark echoed those from U.S. Treasury Undersecretary John Taylor, who said on Monday that measures to speed up global growth would be the focus at the meeting in Boca Raton, Florida.   \par \par \uc2  Still, with the dollar sinking to its lowest level against the yen in nearly 3-= years only a day earlier, threatening to dampen Japan's export-driven economic recovery, Tanigaki sought to avoid being interpreted in the hard-nosed market as softening his stance against a higher yen.   \par \par \uc2  "The issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be neglected by the G7," he said.   \par \par \uc2  Complaints from European officials worried about the euro's rise to record highs against the dollar, which at least on the surface were similar to Japan's stance, fuelled speculation that the G7 would amend its statement to express concern about the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  But more recently, the dollar has come under renewed pressure as signs that Europe was also unhappy about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  led to growing anticipation that any change to the G7 statement, which in September called for greater exchange rate flexibility, would be limited.   \par \par \uc2  RECORD \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Asked if he expected his G7 counterparts to be approving of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record-shattering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past year, Tanigaki said he would not know until they talked about it.   \par \par \uc2  However, his counterparts shared the view that exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals and move in a stable manner, he said.   \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had conducted a monthly record of 7.15 trillion yen ($67.75 billion) of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January, on top of an annual record of 20 trillion yen in 2003.   \par \par \uc2  Despite such huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was hovering just above 105.50 yen in early trading on Tuesday, barely making any headway after falling to around 105.40 yen on Monday, its lowest against the yen in nearly 3 = years.   \par \par \uc2  Japan has been concerned that a higher yen will suffocate its exporting industries and exacerbate the price deflation that has undermined the economy for years.   \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi, who met with his counterpart sherpas from the G7 countries in Brussels last week to set the agenda for the Florida meeting, said \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be part of the economic talks.   \par \par \uc2  "Everyone is looking towards expanding their economies, and so it is important that G7 countries run their economies in an appropriate way," he said when asked how Japan would explain its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  position at the forthcoming G7 meeting.   \par \par \uc2  "Japan is proceeding with structural reform, and monetary policy is very loose. The U.S. is working to lower its deficits. Europe is pushing structural reform and has said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability is very important. In general, there is no difference in views here." ($1=105.53 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040203e0230009k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
