\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar dangles near three-year lows vs yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 2004\par 02:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 2 (Reuters) - The dollar was softer on Monday, dangling barely above last week's three-year lows against the yen amid speculation that the Group of Seven industrial powers may not agree on halting its broad-based decline.   \par \par \uc2  Although the market was cautious after data last Friday showed that Japan spent a record 7.15 trillion yen ($67.2 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January, traders said the dollar's persistent weakness despite such help was a signal of how strong the downward pressure was.   \par \par \uc2  Weaker-than-expected U.S. growth figures for the fourth quarter on Friday also undermined the dollar, even though the U.S. economy was still thought to be performing better than those of Europe and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  "The authorities have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a considerable pace this year, and it seems they are not going to let the dollar fall through 105 yen so easily," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at UFJ Bank.   \par \par \uc2  "So people will be cautious in Tokyo trading hours. But that does not mean the dollar won't come under pressure again in European and U.S. hours."   \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 105.66/69 yen at 0017 GMT, down from around 105.75 yen late in New York on Friday and within sight of three-year lows around 105.45 yen set a week earlier.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said commercial orders, such as dollar-selling by Japanese exporters, were relatively slow.   \par \par \uc2  This, combined with weekly data showing ballooning short dollar positions among the speculative accounts in the Chicago futures market, suggested that it was mainly short-term traders selling the dollar at the moment.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said speculation was growing that the G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs meeting in Florida on Friday and Saturday would be unable to agree on any strong statement aimed at halting the dollar's decline.   \par \par \uc2  Despite growing concern among European and Japanese officials about the dollar's weakness, U.S. authorities were in no hurry to alter a G7 statement issued in September calling for more flexibility in exchange rates, traders said.   \par \par \uc2  "Firstly, the wording may not be changed that much from the Dubai statement. Secondly, there are voices in Europe that say Japan and other Asian countries should shoulder more of the burden of the dollar's adjustment," said Daisuke Uno, market analyst at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp.   \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.2477/82, little changed from late Friday levels.   \par \par \uc2  The market was awaiting a flurry of economic data later in the day, including purchasing management indices from European countries and the U.S. Institute of Supply Management manufacturing index. ($1=105.70 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040202e0220008e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
