\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Treads Water As Mkt Awaits US GDP Data  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Miyako Takebe  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2004\par 06:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar held steady against its major rivals Friday in Asia, taking a breather from its recent rally after encouraging U.S. economic data and the Federal Reserve's more hawkish-sounding outlook for U.S. interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 While holding onto its overnight gains against the euro, the dollar lacked momentum to explore higher ground after the market finished digesting the implications of Wednesday's Federal Open Market Committee statement, Tokyo traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "The market appears to have finally calmed down a bit," said Masamichi Koike, head of spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp.  \par \par \uc2 But expectations that U.S. fourth-quarter gross domestic product, due later in the global session, will show solid growth helped the dollar sustain most of its gains it logged in recent sessions.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had been strengthening against the euro since Wednesday, when the FOMC left its benchmark federal funds rate unchanged at a historic low of 1% but said it "believes that it can be patient in removing its policy accommodation." That was a change from its previous pledge to keep rates low for a "considerable period," and thus led to speculation that a rise in interest rates might come sooner than most observers had previously expected.  \par \par \uc2 SMBC's Koike said while the initial reaction to the statement is wearing off, going forward, it may still act as a catalyst to gradually push down high-interest rate \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the Australian dollar, against the U.S. unit.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar's movements Friday were constrained by persistent selling by Japanese exporters at highs, and expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities at lows - keeping it trapped with a tight 25-sen band, Tokyo traders said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y105.93, little changed from Y105.90 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at Y106.05 late Thursday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2414, a touch higher than $1.2410 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.2465 in Tokyo Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y131.50, up from Y131.42 in New York late Thursday. The euro was at Y132.20 in Tokyo late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.8163, up a tad from $1.8160 in New York but lower than $1.8173 in Tokyo Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 In addition to the U.S. GDP data, Tokyo players await the Japanese Finance Ministry's announcement at 1000 GMT of how much the government spent on yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders estimate the ministry spent more than Y7 trillion, which would far outstrip the previous record of Y4.46 trillion in September. It would also underscore Tokyo's unwavering determination to prevent yen gains from harming exporters, which are still fueling the economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Along with the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amount, the Finance Ministry will announce how much stopgap funding it procured from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January to help finance \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It will do so by releasing the total value of U.S. Treasurys sold to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  under repurchase agreements.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ agreed last month to buy up to Y10 trillion in U.S. Treasurys when needed to help tide the government over while it waits for Parliament approval on new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funding. Analysts speculate that the BOJ has already bought some Y5 trillion worth of Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 But with Parliament expected to clear an extra budget bill for the current fiscal year as early as next week, the government is unlikely to face any serious \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funding shortages. The enactment of the extra budget will give the ministry some Y21 trillion more in funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with.  \par \par \uc2 Later in the Asian session, the euro took a slight tumble on position adjustments caused by lingering uncertainty over how top Group of Seven finance officials will address the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their meeting Feb. 6-7 in the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell below $1.2400 to near $1.2380, while also tumbling to a low of Y131.25 against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a bit scary" buying euros now, said a trader at a Japanese brokerage.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates that accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subscribers can find this table:  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1074  \