\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar firms, buoyed by U.S. economic data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2004\par 17:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 30 (Reuters) - The dollar moved higher against the euro on Friday after reports showing U.S. consumer sentiment remained strong in January and business activity in the U.S. Midwest expanding for a ninth straight month and at a faster pace.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback's advance signaled to some analysts that the U.S. economic panorama was returning to \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders' radar screens as a primary factor driving the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The National Association of Purchasing Management-Chicago index showed a better than expected manufacturing activity in the Midwest in January at 65.9 compared to an upwardly revised 61.2 in December. Analysts polled by Reuters were expecting a reading of 62.0 in January.  \par \par \uc2 "The Chicago PMI was certainly better than expected. The dollar rose further on that. Perhaps short-term dollar gains were mitigated somewhat by the fact that euro/dollar had come down 30 or 40 ticks in the 10 minutes or so prior to the (Chicago) data limiting the extent to which (the euro) was able to move lower," Robert Lynch, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, BNP Paribas, New York.  \par \par \uc2 The University of Michigan's final reading of January's consumer sentiment index showed a stronger than expected 103.8, up from 92.6 in December, supported by lower interest rates, better job prospects and stock market gains fueled by robust earnings. Analysts had expected the January reading at 103.0.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar moved higher after the University of Michigan report, maybe because the Michigan number was revised up even though the revision was quite small. The number was good and maybe the market was a little bit braced for a slight downward revision," Lynch said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy grew at a 4 percent annual rate in the final three months of last year; surprisingly lower than expected, the government reported on Friday. GDP had been expected to show annualized growth of 4.8 percent in the fourth quarter, according to a Reuters poll, following a sizzling 8.2 percent pace in the third quarter.  \par \par \uc2 "We all knew that Q3 couldn't last forever," said Lara Rhame, senior economist \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Brown Brothers Harriman & CO., in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "Four percent is going to feel like a disappointment compared to the consensus and compared to Q3 but it is still a very respectable rate of growth and details show that the recovery is well on track including solid growth, and private investment data," Rhame said.  \par \par \uc2 By mid morning on Friday in New York, the euro was down 0.2 percent at $1.2382 because of the strong U.S. data.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had been rallying since the Federal Reserve surprised the market on Wednesday by dropping its pledge to keep rates low for a "considerable period." The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  replaced that phrase with an assurance that it "can be patient" in removing its policy accommodation.  \par \par \uc2 Financial markets interpreted that shift in language as a signal that the Fed was nearer a rate hike than had been previously thought, giving the dollar a boost and sending both stocks and U.S. Treasuries downward. Official U.S. interest rates remain at 1 percent, their lowest since 1958.  \par \par \uc2 Business conditions in New York City improved for a fifth straight month in January, boosted by a pick-up in the tourism and banking industries, a report published on Friday showed.  \par \par \uc2 The National Association of Purchasing Management index of economic activity in the Big Apple climbed to 257.3 in January from 242.6 in December.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the trading day, the greenback softened a touch on news \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in record amounts of about $67.56 billion in January in an attempt to stem the yen's rise against the dollar from crimping exports, but still couldn't stop the yen from hitting three-year highs earlier this month.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar declined to 105.72 yen, not far above this week's three-year low around 105.42 yen, according to Reuters data. (Additional reporting by John Parry in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040130e01u00w6y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
