\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Japan jobless rate falls, outlook brightens.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tamawa Kadoya  \par \uc2 795 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2004\par 11:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 30 (Reuters) - Japan's jobless rate fell to a two-and-a-half year low in December and deflation showed signs of easing, but spending remained subdued as consumers waited for more evidence that their jobs and salaries were safe.   \par \par \uc2  Data on Friday put the unemployment rate at 4.9 percent, down from 5.2 in November. It was the first time since June 2001 that the number of jobless was below five percent of the workforce.   \par \par \uc2  The better than expected outcome - most economists had expected no change - reinforced expectations that gross domestic product, helped by strong exports, may have grown as much as five percent on an annualised basis in the October-December quarter.   \par \par \uc2  A government official said the unemployment rate, which hit a post-war high of 5.5 percent in January 2003, was expected to keep falling, although at a moderate pace.   \par \par \uc2  "Labour conditions are improving in line with the overall economic recovery, but unemployment among young men remains high," said Michio Matsumura, director of the Labour Force Statistics Office at the Ministry of Public Management.   \par \par \uc2  Most of the pick-up in employment was in the services sector, particularly in the medical and welfare area, but also in the hotel, restaurant and transport industries.   \par \par \uc2  The number of employed rose for the first time in five months while the unemployment rate for calendar 2003 averaged at 5.3 percent, down for the first time in 13 years. The jobless rate among men matched 2003's record high of 5.5 percent, however.   \par \par \uc2  Unemployment remained highest among men aged between 15-24, at 10 percent in December, while agriculture and construction showed the largest falls in the number of workers in the month.   \par \par \uc2  "The unemployment rate clearly broke out of the recent range, suggesting an improvement in labour conditions," said Takahide Kiuchi, an economist at Nomura Research Institute.   \par \par \uc2  "Still, firms are unlikely to stop cutting labour costs. They are unlikely to raise fixed wages, so I don't expect a drastic improvement in income," he said.   \par \par \uc2  SPENDING SUBDUED   \par \par \uc2  Separate data showed wage earners' monthly spending per household in December fell by a real 3.2 percent from November to 383,037 yen ($3,617), seasonally adjusted.   \par \par \uc2  Compared with a year earlier, however, spending was up 1.1 percent, and the 0.2 percent rise in the quarter compared with a year earlier marked the first quarterly rise in five quarters.   \par \par \uc2  An outbreak of mad cow disease in the United States last month took its toll on sales of meat products. With a ban on U.S. beef imports as well as on chicken imports from some Asian countries because of bird flu, meat prices are rising.   \par \par \uc2  But the core nationwide consumer price index was unchanged in the month from a year earlier and deflation in 2003 averaged 0.3 percent, making for a fourth straight year of falling prices.   \par \par \uc2  The persistent fall in consumer prices has somewhat eased from past years, but its debilitating effect still weighs on the economy as Japan tries to emerge from a decade-long slump.   \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan is flooding the markets with liquidity to keep short-term interest rates at zero but demand remains slack, and cheap imports have helped keep downward pressure on prices.   \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui said on Friday that deflation was easing but there was no sign yet of it ending entirely.   \par \par \uc2  He said it was too early to consider exiting its current monetary easing framework, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has pledged to continue until the CPI stabilised above zero.   \par \par \uc2  With robust exports and steady demand at home for electronics goods feeding into production, many economists expect the economic pick-up to continue despite concern about a strong yen.   \par \par \uc2  Industrial production rose 3.6 percent in October-December over the previous quarter, data showed on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  Separate data on Friday showed housing starts rose 9.4 percent in December from a year earlier.   \par \par \uc2  A Reuters survey also showed that manufacturing activity expanded again in January as orders, particularly for electronics, rolled in from home and abroad, albeit at a slower pace.   \par \par \uc2  The headline index in the Reuters/Nomura/JMMA Purchasing Managers Index (PMI) came in at 55.7, down from 56.0 in December but above the crucial 50 level for an eighth consecutive month.   \par \par \uc2  Despite the brighter outlook, authorities remain concerned about the adverse impact of the yen's rise, prompting them to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in recent months.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent 7.1545 trillion yen ($68 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the December 27-January 28 period, a record for a single month, Finance Ministry data showed on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Jonathan Standing, Chisa Fujioka).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040130e01u00uts\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
