\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar steady as G7 nervousness limits active trade.  \b0\par\par\uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2004\par 02:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 30 (Reuters) - The dollar was steady on Friday on growing hopes the U.S. Federal Reserve will raise interest rates sooner rather that later, but the market remained nervous about the outcome of next week's Group of Seven meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Although the dollar was firm across the board, its upside against the yen was limited by yen-buying needs in the cross market, mainly the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said that the end-month selling needs of Japanese exporters and investors were expected to block the greenback's rise around 106.20 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is very nervous ahead of the G7 meeting, with options-related trade making the dollar/yen rate choppy," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  section manager as Shinsei Bank.  \par \par \uc2 At 0053 GMT, the dollar stood at 106.02/07 yen up slightly from the late U.S. level of 105.90/98.  \par \par \uc2 Any sharp drop in the dollar was seen limited on expectations that the Bank of Japan would step in to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the sake of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exporters.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese authorities were thought to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, when the dollar suddenly spiked up by nearly a yen to 106.65 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry is due to release \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures for January later in the day. The numbers are expected to be a single-month record upwards of five trillion yen ($47 billion), traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was steady at 131.54/61 yen but it was sharply off this week's high of around 134.20 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at $1.2404/09 versus late New York's $1.2410/14.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is being bought back ahead of the G7 meeting to unwind some short positions built up over time, and such a trend could continue until the meeting," Shinsei's Watanabe said.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback was also supported after the Fed left its official interest rates steady on Wednesday but surprised the market by dropping its pledge to keep rates low for a "considerable period".  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  replaced that closely watched phrase with an assurance that it can be "patient" before lifting rates.  \par \par \uc2 The market is awaiting fourth-quarter U.S. gross domestic product (GDP) data - due later on Friday - for clues about an official interest-rate hike later this year.  \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters forecast that the Commerce Department's preliminary estimate would show GDP grew 5.8 percent in the final quarter of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 The market showed little reaction to stronger-than-expected Japanese payrolls data released earlier in the morning.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese government on Friday upgraded its view on job conditions, saying they continued to be tough but were showing signs of improvement.  \par \par \uc2 It announced earlier that the unemployment rate was 4.9 percent in December, the lowest in two-and-a-half years and down from November's 5.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Economists in a Reuters poll last week had produced a median forecast of 5.2 percent. Unemployment reached a record high of 5.5 percent in January 2003. ($1=105.88 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040130e01u005c8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
