\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Scaling New Peaks Again  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,030 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2004\par 20:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  confirmed Friday what \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants already knew: its yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this month reached record levels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The sheer scale of the numbers underscore the ministry's determination not to allow the yen to strengthen to an extent that would derail Japan's export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The nation's monetary authorities spent a whopping Y7.16 trillion on the global foreign exchanges in the period from Dec 27 through Jan 28 in an effort to stop the yen from strengthening this month. This easily outstrips the previous monthly record of Y5.11 trillion in September last year and is almost a third of last year's total of Y20.05 trillion, itself a record for one year.  \par \par \uc2 If it were to continue at this rate, Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year would total a fifth of the country's entire $4 trillion annual gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 But although policy makers in Europe and the U.S. may complain bitterly at this perceived manipulation to secure a competitive advantage for Japan's exporters, Japanese officials will be able to look them straight in the eye at the Feb. 6-7 meetings of Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers.  \par \par \uc2 "It's an issue but I don't think it's the main focus," said Derek Halpenny, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi in London.  \par \par \uc2 As long as Japan can show that it's tackling the huge non-performing loans weighing on its banking sector and doing all it can to fight off deflation and boost demand and economic growth, then the other delegates around the G7 table can have little cause for complaint. "Besides, it's the only policy option they have available," Halpenny added.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Reflating Japan's Economy \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese interest rates are virtually at zero so authorities have to take other reflationary measures to try to boost the economy. Last week the Bank of Japan raised its liquidity target under its quantitative easing policy by Y3 trillion to a new range of Y30 trillion to Y35 trillion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer price data Friday showed the world's second largest economy is still suffering from deflation. For all of 2003, core consumer prices fell 0.3%, decreasing for the fourth straight year, and the overall CPI dropped 0.3% to extend its declining streak to a fifth year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In this context, Japan's "continuous monetary easing...is very welcome," said John Chambers, deputy head of Standard & Poor's sovereign ratings on Thursday. Japan is "turning the corner" in its fight against deflation and the easing is "taking some of the pressure off" Japan's non-performing loans, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With domestic demand accounting for only 55% of GDP, Japan relies heavily on its export sector for overall economic growth. Exports themselves account for only around 12%-14% of GDP. But net exports are the biggest single contributory factor to quarter-on-quarter GDP growth, around 50% of overall growth, noted Halpenny at BoTM.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow himself reiterated earlier this week that promoting growth will top the G7 agenda. And as the world's second largest economy, it's in everyone's interests that Japan grows.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone policy makers are becoming increasingly vocal in their protests at the strength of the euro, which they believe is shouldering a disproportionately large share of the burden of the dollar's weakness because \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and other Asian central banks are \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so heavily.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Whatever happened, they'll likely argue, to the "flexibility" in exchange rates explicitly called for in the last G7 communique from Dubai last September?  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Appears To Be Working \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The response from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - and the U.S. - could be to point out that if euro-zone officials want to make the euro more competitive and do their part to boost economic growth, the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should simply cut interest rates. With benchmark rates at 2%, it arguably has some scope to do so.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Criticism from U.S. officials at Japan's heavy-handedness in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets might well be tempered by the recognition that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is a major pillar in propping up the U.S. Treasury market. Japan recycles the dollars bought in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets via purchases of U.S. government securities. With over $500 billion in Treasurys, it's by far the biggest single holder of U.S. government debt.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So, has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  been successful? On one level, it hasn't. This week, the dollar weakened to a new three and a half year-low against the yen of Y105.47.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But on another level it certainly has, analysts think. The pace of yen appreciation against the dollar has been slower than that of the euro, for example. And Japan's aim isn't to reverse the yen's ascent - although it would no doubt like to - but to slow it. In that sense, one can only speculate as to where the dollar would be if Japan's monetary authorities hadn't been spending so much money in the markets over the past year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think it would be around the all-time lows just below Y80," said Neil Jones, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sales at Nomura International in London.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He reckons that while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue this year, it won't be in such aggressive amounts. Pressure from G7 counterparts "could take its toll," while a healthier domestic economy and equity markets will remove the need for such heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But scaling back could weaken the U.S. Treasury market, send bond yields sharply higher and help slow growth in the U.S., Japan's biggest export market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Is that in (Japan's) interest?" asked one executive at a large bank in New York asked rhetorically.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040130e01u0002g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
