\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Sells Off On Weaker-Than-Expected US GDP Data  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2004\par 14:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar was sold off sharply to intraday lows early Friday in New York after the release of weaker-than-expected fourth-quarter U.S. economic growth data.  \par \par \uc2 Gross domestic product rose 4.0% in the quarter, a slowdown from the torrid 8.2% pace in the third quarter. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC had forecast a 4.8% annual rate of increase.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, which had been rallying in recent days and was trading modestly higher on the day versus European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  heading into the data, tumbled quickly after the number.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell by more than a cent against the euro and pound before regaining its feet a bit.  \par \par \uc2 "The market had gotten a little overly long dollars, and now we'll see what happens," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 While the GDP report was below expectations, it doesn't really change views about the outlook for the U.S. economy, interest rates or the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said analysts.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not bad, but it's simply not as good as the market had expected," said Robert Lynch, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York. "It's a bit disappointing and is sparking a bit of profit-taking after a week of posting good gains."  \par \par \uc2 Lynch said the market's positioning ahead of the release of the data - with the dollar near its highs for the week against most of its major rivals - primed the greenback for a sharp selloff if the numbers failed to match expectations.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had been surging in the wake of Wednesday's decision by Federal Reserve policy makers to drop the phrase that monetary policy will remain accommodative for a "considerable period," while leaving rates unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 The statement raised the possibility that the Fed would begin tightening more quickly than expected, which could help the dollar by making U.S. yields relatively more attractive globally.  \par \par \uc2 However, the growth data quickly put a stop to the dollar's gains, which had extended modestly overnight.  \par \par \uc2 "It may take some pressure off the downside in the euro for today," added McCarthy, noting that trading activity could be hard to predict given that it's a Friday and the end of the month. He said the euro may maintain its recent range between $1.2530 and $1.2350.  \par \par \uc2 Early Friday in New York, the euro was trading around $1.2462, up from $1.2410 late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was lower at CHF1.2549 versus CHF1.2595, while sterling rose to $1.8219 from $1.8160.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was lower against the yen, as well, at Y105.73 from Y105.95 late Thursday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar continued to recoup some of its losses as the morning progressed. Around 9:20 a.m. EST (1420 GMT), the euro was trading around $1.2434, up modestly from $1.2410 late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was still lower against the Swiss franc, at CHF1.2575 versus CHF1.2595, while sterling was holding on at $1.8183 from $1.8160.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar remained under pressure against the yen, at Y105.68 from Y105.95 late Thursday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The recent gains in the dollar following the Fed decision were already being viewed as overdone. Considering that rates were left unchanged, the statement didn't alter most economists' outlook on interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Peter Frank, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at ABN-Amro Bank in Chicago, said the benign inflation and weak consumer spending indicated by the GDP data would be the key figures for the Fed.  \par \par \uc2 "This isn't going to push the Fed into tightening policy as soon as the market might have expected," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, Citigroup recommended shorting the dollar against sterling and the New Zealand dollar, whose central banks have already shifted into a tightening cycle.  \par \par \uc2 Investors may also remain wary being too long the dollar ahead of next week's meeting of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations, since the U.S. is unlikely to support any change in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, say analysts.  \par \par \uc2 No change in the G7 communique "is likely to come as a modest disappointment to market participants," said Citibank analysts in a research note. Thus, the combination of no official U.S. support for a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , abundant liquidity from low interest rates, and worsening trade deficits "appears a recipe for the (dollar) to fall towards new lows."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar is expected to remain under pressure against the yen, with Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporting Friday that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling a record Y7.15 billion over the past month.  \par \par \uc2 The amount, which was spent during the period between Dec. 29 to Jan. 28, was more than a third of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's total Y20.1 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during all of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was sold overnight on the data because it indicates the level of effort Japan faces in keeping the yen from appreciating too quickly and threatening the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, "the market continues to expect the BOJ to allow (dollar/yen) to weaken ahead of the G7 meeting," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Bank in New York, in a research note. HSBC still expects the dollar to end the quarter lower at Y103.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385; tom.barkley@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Jamie McGeever contributed to this story.)  \par \par \uc2 1074 1863 28038 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040130e01u00010\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalp